TP.register({id:"lp10-g6",lp:10,grade:"6",mode:"curriculum",checks:9,nextGrade:"7th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="The Last Assembly";const STORY=`<p>Octavio had the slideshow on a flash drive in his pocket for six days before the assembly, and he checked that it was still there about forty times.</p>
<p>He had built it over three weeks: 118 photographs, in order, with the names underneath, which took longer than finding the photographs did. Twice he had to go back and fix a name he had spelled from memory.</p>
<p>The gym was already hot when they filed in. Somebody had propped the doors, which helped the air and ruined the sound. The chorus went first, and nobody past the fourth row heard a word of it.</p>
<p>Then the sound system cut out.</p>
<p>For ten minutes nothing happened that anybody could do anything about. Four hundred students sat in a gym with no microphone, and the noise they made filling that silence was louder than anything that had been on the stage.</p>
<p>When the power came back the principal looked at the clock, looked at the program, and said the slideshow would have to wait until September. The assembly had been given an hour and had already taken twenty-five minutes more than that.</p>
<p>Octavio put the flash drive back in his pocket.</p>
<p>In September, on the second day, a seventh grader he barely knew stopped him in the hallway and asked whether he still had it. Then another one did. By the end of that week the office had put the slideshow on the hallway screen, where it ran on a loop for nine days, and more people watched it there than would ever have fit in the gym.</p>`;const story=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${STORY_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${STORY}</div>`;const POEM_TITLE="Last Bell";const POEM_SRC="a poem by Renata Alvear";const POEM=`<p>The year does not end at 3:05.<br>
It ends in pieces, over a week,<br>
the way a tide goes out:<br>
the locker emptied Tuesday,<br>
the library card returned Wednesday,<br>
the last real lesson somewhere in there,<br>
unannounced, already over<br>
by the time you notice.</p>
<p>They give you a bell for it,<br>
and a gym, and a folding chair,<br>
as if a year were a thing<br>
you could put down all at once.</p>
<p>What you carry out<br>
is not the bell.<br>
It is the name of a river<br>
you learned in October<br>
and have not forgotten yet.</p>`;const poem=()=>`<article><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${POEM_TITLE}</h3><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 8px">${POEM_SRC}</p><div class="passage sm">${POEM}</div></article>`;const GYM_TITLE="Why a Gym Is Hard to Hear In";const GYM=`<p>A school gym is one of the hardest rooms in a building to hear a voice in, and the reason is the surfaces.</p>
<p>Sound leaves a speaker in every direction. Some of it reaches your ear directly. The rest hits the floor, the ceiling and the walls, and bounces. In a room with carpet, curtains and padded seats, most of that bounced sound is soaked up. In a gym almost none of it is: polished wood, painted block and a metal roof all send sound back instead of absorbing it.</p>
<p>The bounced sound arrives a fraction of a second late, so one voice becomes a smear of overlapping copies. Consonants suffer most, because they are quick and quiet, and they are what tell one word from another. Engineers measure the problem with reverberation time, the number of seconds a sound takes to fade away. A classroom aims for under 0.6 seconds. A full gym can run to 2 seconds or more.</p>
<p>A crowd helps, oddly enough. People absorb sound, and a gym with several hundred people in it is noticeably clearer than the same gym empty. That is why a sound check in an empty room tells you very little about what the room will do once it is full.</p>`;const gym=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${GYM_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${GYM}</div>`;const COL_TITLE="Give the Hour Back";const COL_SRC="Opinion column by Hollis Trent, eighth grade, in the Linden Park student paper, May 2026.";const COL=`<p>Here is what the end-of-year assembly actually is. Four hundred people are put in a hot room with bad sound, told to sit still on folding chairs for an hour, and then kept there for twenty-five minutes longer than that. Call it what you like. It is a line.</p>
<p>I am not against marking the end of the year. I am against marking it in the one room in this building where nobody can hear anything, for the one reason that it is the only room we all fit in.</p>
<p>Think about what we gave up last June. The chorus sang and nobody past the fourth row heard them. The sound died for ten minutes. The slideshow, which somebody spent three weeks building, got cut, and then ran on the hallway screen in September, where more people watched it than ever would have seen it in the gym.</p>
<p>That last part should tell us something. The thing people actually wanted was the slideshow, and the gym was the part that got in its way.</p>
<p>Give us the hour back. Put the slideshow on the screens, put the games on the field, and let the year end somewhere people can hear it.</p>`;const column=()=>`<article><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${COL_TITLE}</h3><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 8px">${COL_SRC}</p><div class="passage sm">${COL}</div></article>`;const A_SRC="Account A: from the Linden Park school newsletter, June 2026. Written by the office.";const A_TEXT=`<p>The school closed the year on Friday with its annual assembly in the gym. The sixth-grade chorus opened with two pieces they had rehearsed since March. Ms. Ferraro's committee had spent eight weeks on the program, and twenty-two staff members gave up a planning period to run it.</p>
<p>A power interruption affected the sound system briefly during the middle of the program. The remaining items were completed, and because of the delay the sixth-grade slideshow was held over to the fall, where it was shown on the hallway screens in September and reached an even wider audience.</p>
<p>Thank you to everyone who made the morning possible.</p>`;const B_SRC="Account B: from a blog kept by Mireille Dos Santos, a sixth grader at Linden Park, June 2026.";const B_TEXT=`<p>It was 84 degrees in the gym. They propped the doors, which made it bearable and made the sound worse, and the chorus sang for nine minutes into a room where nobody past row four heard a note of it.</p>
<p>Then the sound went out. Ten minutes. Four hundred people with nothing to listen to, in a hot room, on folding chairs. I timed it, because there was nothing else to do.</p>
<p>We were there an hour and twenty-five minutes for a thing that was scheduled for an hour. The slideshow, which was the only part I actually wanted, got cut at the end because we had run over.</p>
<p>It did run in September, on the hallway screen, and it was good. I am glad about that. I would still like the twenty-five minutes back.</p>`;const twoAccounts=()=>`<style>body:not(.sup-big) .texts article p{font-size:1.06rem;line-height:1.5}</style><div class="texts"><article><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${A_SRC}</p>${A_TEXT}</article><article><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${B_SRC}</p>${B_TEXT}</article></div>`;const SRC_A_CARD="Report: “Speech Clarity in School Multi-Purpose Rooms: Measurements at 14 Sites.” By Dr. Priyanka Raval, acoustical engineer, Westmoor Acoustics Laboratory, 2024.";const SRC_A_TEXT="Across the fourteen gyms measured, reverberation time with the room empty averaged 2.4 seconds. Adding an audience of 300 or more brought the average down to 1.3 seconds. Word recognition scores for listeners seated beyond row six improved by 31 percent once the room was full.";const SRC_B_CARD="A comment on a school sports message board by a user named gym_guy_77. No date.";const SRC_B_TEXT="Honestly I think the echo thing is overblown. Just buy a louder speaker. More volume = more clear, thats basic physics.";const srcCard=(tag,head,body)=>`<article style="flex:1 1 320px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${tag}</h3><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${body}</p></article>`;const SOURCES=()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Research question: would a louder speaker fix the sound in the gym?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${srcCard("Source A",SRC_A_CARD,SRC_A_TEXT)}${srcCard("Source B",SRC_B_CARD,SRC_B_TEXT)}</div>`;const COUNCIL_READ=["Report of the Student Council End of Year Committee.","The committee was asked to recommend how Linden Park should mark the last day of school. We surveyed all 180 sixth graders and we read the two written accounts of last June's assembly.","We recommend splitting the day. Keep a short gathering, no longer than twenty minutes, for the awards and the thank-yous, which genuinely need everybody in one room. Move everything that depends on being heard or seen in detail, including the slideshow, onto the hallway screens, where it ran for nine days last September and reached more people than the gym holds.","Then give the rest of the time to the field.","One caution. Our survey covers sixth graders only. We did not ask the seventh or eighth grade, and we did not ask the staff, so the numbers below should not be read as what the whole school wants."].join("\n\n");const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const bigTable=(head,rows,cap)=>`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:14px auto;font-size:1.3rem">${cap?`<caption style="font-size:1.1rem;padding-bottom:6px">${cap}</caption>`:""}<tr>${head.map(h=>`<th style="text-align:center">${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr>${rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map((c,i)=>i?`<td style="text-align:center">${c}</td>`:`<th>${c}</th>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div>`;const lead=t=>`<p class="big">${t}</p>`;const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:16px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.15rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const SURVEY=[["Field games outside",72],["Half day, go home early",50],["Assembly in the gym",44],["No opinion",14]];const SURVEY_N=SURVEY.reduce((a,r)=>a+r[1],0);function bars(o){const n=o.cats.length,rows=Math.round(o.max/o.step),cell=o.cell||34,x0=70,y0=34,bw=o.bw||176,W=x0+n*bw+24,H=y0+rows*cell+58;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${H}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="Bar graph: ${o.title}">`;s+=`<text x="${x0+n*bw/2}" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${o.title}</text>`;for(let r=0;r<=rows;r++){const y=y0+(rows-r)*cell;s+=`<line x1="${x0}" y1="${y}" x2="${x0+n*bw}" y2="${y}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2"/>`;s+=`<text x="${x0-10}" y="${y}" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" font-size="15" fill="var(--ink)">${r*o.step}</text>`}o.cats.forEach((c,i)=>{const h=o.vals[i]/o.max*rows*cell,bx=x0+i*bw+bw*.18;s+=`<rect x="${bx}" y="${y0+rows*cell-h}" width="${bw*.64}" height="${h}" fill="var(--sea)" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="2"/>`;s+=`<text x="${bx+bw*.32}" y="${y0+rows*cell-h-9}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${o.vals[i]}</text>`;s+=`<text x="${bx+bw*.32}" y="${y0+rows*cell+26}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${c}</text>`});return s+"</svg>"}const surveyChart=()=>bars({title:"What should the last day be? "+SURVEY_N+" sixth graders asked",cats:SURVEY.map(r=>r[0]),vals:SURVEY.map(r=>r[1]),max:80,step:20,bw:186});const surveyTable=()=>bigTable(["Choice","Students","Share of the 180"],SURVEY.map(r=>[r[0],r[1],Math.round(r[1]/SURVEY_N*1e3)/10+"%"]),"Sixth-grade survey, all 180 asked and all 180 answered");const SETUP=[12,15,15,18,20,22,25,25,28,30,65];function dotPlot(vals,o){const min=o.min,max=o.max,W=o.width||740,x0=56,x1=W-34,base=156,X=v=>x0+(v-min)/(max-min)*(x1-x0);const counts={};vals.forEach(v=>counts[v]=(counts[v]||0)+1);const H=base+60;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${H}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt}">`;s+=`<line x1="${x0-18}" y1="${base}" x2="${x1+18}" y2="${base}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/>`;for(let v=min;v<=max;v+=o.tick){const x=X(v),big=(v-min)%o.label===0;s+=`<line x1="${x}" y1="${base-(big?10:6)}" x2="${x}" y2="${base+(big?10:6)}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="${big?2.6:1.8}"/>`;if(big)s+=`<text x="${x}" y="${base+32}" font-size="16" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${v}</text>`}Object.keys(counts).forEach(k=>{const v=+k;for(let i=0;i<counts[k];i++)s+=`<circle cx="${X(v)}" cy="${base-18-i*20}" r="7.5" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`});s+=`<text x="${W/2}" y="${H-10}" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.xl}</text>`;return s+"</svg>"}const setupPlot=()=>dotPlot(SETUP,{min:10,max:70,tick:5,label:10,alt:"Dot plot of setup times in minutes for eleven classes, from 10 to 70 minutes. Most dots sit between 12 and 30, and one dot sits alone at 65.",xl:"Minutes to set up a game station"});function boxFig(len,dep,hei){const T=(x,y,t,a)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="3.5">${t}</text>`;return`<svg viewBox="0 0 360 216" width="360" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="A box drawn in three dimensions with the front edge labeled ${len}, the depth labeled ${dep}, and the height labeled ${hei}">
   <polygon points="86,70 242,70 290,34 134,34" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <polygon points="242,70 290,34 290,138 242,174" fill="var(--gold)" fill-opacity=".4" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <rect x="86" y="70" width="156" height="104" fill="var(--sea-soft)" fill-opacity=".6" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <line x1="296" y1="50" x2="273" y2="47" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
   ${T(164,198,len)}${T(80,126,hei,"end")}${T(300,56,dep,"start")}</svg>`}function lZoneFig(){const u=14,ox=60,oy=28,T=(x,y,t,a)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${t}</text>`;const P=[[0,0],[14,0],[14,5],[20,5],[20,12],[0,12]].map(p=>`${ox+p[0]*u},${oy+p[1]*u}`).join(" ");return`<svg viewBox="0 0 410 240" width="410" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="An L shaped zone. The whole rectangle would be 20 feet along the bottom and 12 feet down the left side, and a piece 6 feet wide and 5 feet tall has been taken out of the top right corner."><polygon points="${P}" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linejoin="round"/>`+T(ox+7*u,oy-6,"14 ft")+T(ox+14*u+8,oy+2.5*u,"5 ft","start")+T(ox+17*u,oy+5*u-8,"6 ft")+T(ox+20*u+8,oy+8.5*u,"7 ft","start")+T(ox+10*u,oy+12*u+26,"20 ft")+T(ox-8,oy+6*u,"12 ft","end")+"</svg>"}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"The Last Assembly",short:"Assembly",parts:[{std:["RL.6.1"],dur:115,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that support each answer.",desc:"citing textual evidence for what a story says and for inferences drawn from it",html:story,qs:["How long did Octavio spend building the slideshow, and what part of it took the longest? Cite the evidence.","The story never says Octavio was anxious about losing the slideshow. What evidence shows that he was?","Why did the principal cut the slideshow? Cite the strongest evidence in the story.","What does the last paragraph suggest about where the slideshow belonged all along? Cite the evidence."],key:["Three weeks, and the names under the photographs took longer than finding the photographs. Cites “He had built it over three weeks: 118 photographs, in order, with the names underneath, which took longer than finding the photographs did.” (RL.6.1, explicit evidence)","He carried it in his pocket for six days and “checked that it was still there about forty times.” He also “put the flash drive back in his pocket” rather than leaving it with anyone. (RL.6.1, inference supported by evidence)","Because the assembly had already run far over its time. Cites “the principal looked at the clock, looked at the program,” and “The assembly had been given an hour and had already taken twenty-five minutes more than that.” (RL.6.1, strongest evidence)","That it did not need the gym at all, and reached people better without it. Cites “it ran on a loop for nine days, and more people watched it there than would ever have fit in the gym,” together with the two students who asked him for it in September. (RL.6.1, inference with evidence)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Eleven Setups",short:"Data",parts:[{std:["6.SP.B.5"],dur:150,dir:"Use the dot plot and the list. Work on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit in every answer.",desc:"summarizing a numerical data set: the number of observations, the attribute and its units, center, variability, the overall pattern, and a striking deviation",html:()=>lead("Eleven sixth-grade classes each set up one game station on the field. Somebody timed every class with a stopwatch and wrote down the minutes it took.")+bigTable(["Minutes, in order"],[[SETUP.join(", ")]],"The eleven setup times"),fig:()=>F.row([setupPlot(),"Every class on one scale"]),qs:["How many observations are in this data set? What exactly was measured, and in what units?","Find the median. Say what it means here.","Find the mean. Show your work.","Find the interquartile range. Show the two quartiles you used.","Describe the overall pattern of the data, and name any striking deviation from it.","Which measure of center describes a typical class better here, the mean or the median? Use the shape of the data to explain."],key:["11 observations, one per class. The attribute is how long a class took to set up its game station, measured with a stopwatch, in minutes. Partial: the count right with no unit named is approaching. (6.SP.B.5a and 6.SP.B.5b)","The median is 22 minutes. With 11 values in order, the median is the sixth one. It means half the classes took 22 minutes or less and half took 22 or more. Or: count in from both ends to the 6th of the 11 values. Partial: 22 given with no account of what it means is approaching. (6.SP.B.5c)","The mean is 25 minutes. The eleven values add to 275, and 275 ÷ 11 = 25. (6.SP.B.5c)","The interquartile range is 13 minutes. The lower half is 12, 15, 15, 18, 20, so the first quartile is 15. The upper half is 25, 25, 28, 30, 65, so the third quartile is 28. 28 − 15 = 13. Partial: both quartiles right with no subtraction is approaching. (6.SP.B.5c, a measure of variability)","Most classes cluster between 12 and 30 minutes, with the thickest part in the high teens and low twenties. One class is a striking deviation at 65 minutes, more than twice the next longest and well away from every other dot on the plot. Partial: the cluster described with the 65 not singled out is approaching. (6.SP.B.5c, overall pattern and striking deviation in context)","The median, 22 minutes. The data are bunched at the low end with one value far out to the right, and a single extreme value drags the mean toward it: without the 65 the mean would be 21 minutes, close to the median. The median barely moves, so it describes a typical class better. Partial: naming the median with no use of the shape of the data is approaching. (6.SP.B.5d, relating the choice of measure to the shape of the distribution)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Why a Gym Is Hard to Hear In",short:"The Gym",parts:[{std:["RI.6.1"],dur:115,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that support each answer.",desc:"citing textual evidence for what an informational text says and for inferences drawn from it",html:gym,qs:["What makes a gym harder to hear in than a carpeted room? Cite the evidence.","What is reverberation time, and what figures does the article give for a classroom and for a gym? Cite the words.","Why does the article say consonants suffer most? Cite the evidence.","The article never says that a sound check done before an audience arrives can give a false result. Cite the evidence that shows it would."],key:["The surfaces. Cites “polished wood, painted block and a metal roof all send sound back instead of absorbing it,” against a room where “carpet, curtains and padded seats” soak the bounced sound up. (RI.6.1, explicit evidence)","The number of seconds a sound takes to fade away. Cites “A classroom aims for under 0.6 seconds. A full gym can run to 2 seconds or more.” (RI.6.1)","Cites “they are quick and quiet, and they are what tell one word from another,” so the late bounced sound buries exactly the part that distinguishes words. (RI.6.1)","Cites “People absorb sound, and a gym with several hundred people in it is noticeably clearer than the same gym empty,” and “a sound check in an empty room tells you very little about what the room will do once it is full.” Put together, a check run in the empty room measures a different room from the one the audience will sit in. (RI.6.1, inference supported by evidence)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Word by Word",short:"Word Choice",parts:[{std:["RL.6.4"],dur:110,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the words you are talking about.",desc:"figurative and connotative meanings of words and phrases, and the effect of specific word choices on meaning and tone",html:story,qs:["“Somebody had propped the doors, which helped the air and ruined the sound.” Compare what ruined suggests with what a word like changed would suggest. Why did the writer choose ruined?","“the noise they made filling that silence was louder than anything that had been on the stage.” What does that sentence actually tell you about the assembly, and what is its tone?","The writer says he checked the flash drive “about forty times” instead of saying many times. What does the exact number do?","“where it ran on a loop for nine days.” What does on a loop mean here, and what does it suggest about how people saw the slideshow?"],key:["Changed is neutral and would leave the reader to decide whether it mattered. Ruined says the sound was wrecked, not merely altered, and it carries blame and loss. The writer uses it to set up the sentence right after it, where nobody past the fourth row hears the chorus, so the choice makes the propped doors the start of the problem rather than a harmless detail. (RL.6.4, connotative meaning and the effect of word choice)","It tells you that the only thing anybody could hear that morning was the audience itself, so the assembly failed at the one thing it was for. The tone is dry and a little bitter rather than angry: the sentence states the comparison flatly and lets the reader draw the conclusion. (RL.6.4, the effect of a specific word choice on meaning and tone)","A number that precise is obviously an estimate said as a count, which is funnier and more exact at once. It shows the checking was compulsive and that Octavio was aware of it himself, and it makes the loss later land harder. Many times would have passed without the reader noticing. (RL.6.4, the impact of a specific word choice)","On a loop means it played over and over automatically, with no start time and nobody introducing it. It suggests people watched it when they happened to pass, in their own time and as often as they liked, which is the opposite of four hundred people seated at once in one room. (RL.6.4, meaning of a phrase as used in the text)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Pack the Prop Boxes",short:"Volume",parts:[{std:["6.G.A.2"],dur:130,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Show the fractions you multiplied, and say the unit in every answer.",desc:"finding the volume of a box with fractional edge lengths, by packing it with unit fraction cubes and by formula",html:()=>lead("The field-games crew packs its props into boxes. Most of the measurements come out as fractions.")+bigTable(["Edge","Length"],[["Front edge","3 1/2 ft"],["Depth","2 ft"],["Height","1 1/2 ft"]],"Prop box 1"),fig:()=>F.row([boxFig("3 1/2 ft","2 ft","1 1/2 ft"),"Prop box 1"]),qs:["Find the volume of Prop box 1.","How many cubes with edges 1/2 foot long fit inside Prop box 1? Show that your count agrees with the volume you found.","A second box has a base with an area of 9 1/3 square inches and a height of 3 inches. What is its volume?","A flat box is 1/3 yard by 2/3 yard by 1 yard. What is its volume?","A box has a volume of 7 1/2 cubic feet. Its base is 2 1/2 feet by 2 feet. How tall is it?","How many cubes with edges 1/4 inch long fit inside a cube that is 1 inch on every edge?"],key:["10 1/2 cubic feet. V = l × w × h = 7/2 × 2 × 3/2 = 42/4 = 21/2 = 10 1/2. Or: 3.5 × 2 × 1.5 = 10.5 in decimals. (6.G.A.2, applying V = lwh with fractional edges)","84 cubes. Along the edges the box is 7 half-feet by 4 half-feet by 3 half-feet, and 7 × 4 × 3 = 84. Each cube has a volume of 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/8 of a cubic foot, so 84 × 1/8 = 84/8 = 10 1/2 cubic feet, which matches. Or: count by layers, 28 cubes in each of 3 layers. Partial: 84 cubes right with no link to the volume is approaching. (6.G.A.2, packing with unit fraction cubes)","28 cubic inches. V = B × h = 28/3 × 3 = 28. Or: 9 × 3 + 1/3 × 3 = 27 + 1 = 28, splitting the mixed number. (6.G.A.2, applying V = Bh)","2/9 of a cubic yard. V = 1/3 × 2/3 × 1 = 2/9. Or: 1/3 × 2/3 is 2/9, and multiplying by 1 leaves it unchanged. (6.G.A.2)","1 1/2 feet. The base area is 2 1/2 × 2 = 5 square feet, and 15/2 ÷ 5 = 3/2 = 1 1/2. Or: 7.5 ÷ 5 = 1.5 in decimals. (6.G.A.2, an unknown edge)","64 cubes. Four quarter-inch cubes fit along each edge, and 4 × 4 × 4 = 64. Each has a volume of 1/64 of a cubic inch, and 64 × 1/64 = 1 cubic inch. Partial: 64 cubes right with no value for one cube is approaching. (6.G.A.2)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Whose Side Is This",short:"Point of View",parts:[{std:["RI.6.6"],dur:110,dir:"Read the column to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the words that show it.",desc:"determining an author's point of view and purpose and explaining how the text conveys it",html:column,qs:["What is Hollis Trent's point of view about the end-of-year assembly? Be precise: what is she against and what is she not against?","What is her purpose in writing? What does she want the reader to do?","Name two choices in the writing that convey her point of view, and read me the words.","She writes, “Call it what you like. It is a line.” How does that sentence work on the reader, and what does it reveal about where she stands?"],key:["She is against holding the end-of-year event in the gym, because the gym cannot be heard in and is only chosen for being the one room that fits everybody. She is explicitly not against marking the end of the year at all. Reads “I am not against marking the end of the year. I am against marking it in the one room in this building where nobody can hear anything.” (RI.6.6, determining point of view precisely)","To persuade the school to change the format: short gathering out, slideshow on the screens, games on the field. Reads “Give us the hour back. Put the slideshow on the screens, put the games on the field.” It is an opinion column, so the purpose is persuasion rather than report. (RI.6.6, purpose)","Any two, with the words. Opening with the assembly described in the harshest plain terms: “Four hundred people are put in a hot room with bad sound, told to sit still on folding chairs for an hour.” The passive voice in “are put,” which makes students things done to rather than people taking part. The short blunt sentences: “Call it what you like. It is a line.” The imperative ending: “Give us the hour back.” Stating the opposing position first and then narrowing her own claim. (RI.6.6, how the point of view is conveyed)","It refuses the official name for the event and replaces it with a flat, unflattering one, daring the reader to argue. The two very short sentences slow the paragraph and land the word line on its own. It reveals that she thinks the assembly is defended mainly by what it is called rather than by what it does, so taking the name away is most of her argument. (RI.6.6, how word choice conveys point of view)"]}]},{id:7,title:"A Story and a Poem",short:"Compare",parts:[{std:["RL.6.9"],dur:115,dir:"Read both. They are different kinds of writing about the same idea. Then answer out loud.",desc:"comparing and contrasting a story and a poem in their approaches to a similar theme",html:()=>`<div class="texts"><article>${story()}</article><article>${poem()}</article></div>`,qs:["Name a theme that both the story and the poem are about. State it as a full idea, not one word.","How does each one approach that theme differently? Think about what a story can do that a poem cannot, and the other way round.","Both say something about the gym and the folding chairs. Compare what each one does with that detail.","Each ends on something that outlasts the day itself. Compare the two endings and say what each suggests."],key:["Any theme stated as a full idea and supported by both, such as: the official way an ending is marked is not where the ending is actually felt, or what you keep from a year is not the ceremony that was arranged for it. (RL.6.9, a similar theme in two forms)","The story works through one person and one morning in order, so the theme arrives as something that happens to Octavio and is proved by the September outcome. The poem has no characters and no plot: it works by images and a comparison, the tide going out, and it states the idea directly in “as if a year were a thing you could put down all at once.” The story shows, over time; the poem compresses and names. (RL.6.9, contrasting approaches of two genres)","The story puts the reader in the gym for ten silent minutes with four hundred people and makes the room the cause of the failure, with heat, propped doors and dead sound. The poem lists “a bell, and a gym, and a folding chair” in one quick line, as a set of props handed over, and does not describe them at all. One makes the detail an experience, the other makes it a symbol. (RL.6.9)","The story ends with the slideshow running on a hallway screen for nine days and reaching more people than the gym would hold, so what lasts is the thing the ceremony could not contain. The poem ends with the name of a river learned in October and not yet forgotten, so what lasts is private and small and has nothing to do with the ceremony at all. Both reject the bell as the real ending, but the story keeps a public outcome and the poem keeps a private one. (RL.6.9)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Two Accounts",short:"Accounts",parts:[{std:["RI.6.9"],dur:115,dir:"Read both accounts to yourself. They describe the same morning. Then answer out loud.",desc:"comparing and contrasting one author's presentation of events with another author's presentation of the same events",html:twoAccounts,qs:["Name three facts both accounts agree on.","Name two things one account includes that the other leaves out, and say what the choice does.","Both describe the same sound failure. Compare the words each one uses for it.","Who wrote each account, and how does that explain the difference in what each one emphasizes?"],key:["Any three: the assembly happened in the gym on the last Friday; the chorus performed; the sound system failed partway through; the sixth-grade slideshow was not shown that day; the slideshow ran on the hallway screens in September and reached more people. Both accounts carry all of these. (RI.6.9, shared facts)","Any two with the effect. Account A includes the eight weeks of committee work and the twenty-two staff who gave up a planning period, which makes the morning a large voluntary effort. Account B includes the 84 degrees, the ten timed minutes, the nine minutes of unheard singing and the twenty-five minutes over, which makes the morning a cost paid by the people sitting down. Each leaves out what the other counts. (RI.6.9, what each author selects)","Account A: “A power interruption affected the sound system briefly during the middle of the program,” which is impersonal, names no length, and calls it brief. Account B: “Then the sound went out. Ten minutes. Four hundred people with nothing to listen to,” which is short, timed, and told from a seat in the room. The same event is a line of administration in one and an ordeal in the other. (RI.6.9, contrasting presentation)","Account A is written by the office for the school newsletter, so its job is to thank people and keep the school's work in view, which is why staff effort is foregrounded and the failure is softened. Account B is written by a sixth grader on her own blog for herself and her friends, so she is free to report the heat, the timing and her own irritation. Neither is lying; they are answerable to different readers. (RI.6.9, the author's position explains the presentation)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Pronoun Work",short:"Grammar",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.6.1"],dur:60,dir:"Each line has one mistake. Say the whole line out loud the correct way, then name what was wrong.",desc:"pronoun case, shifts in pronoun number and person, and vague pronouns",qs:["The council gave the microphone to Dani and I.","Us sixth graders wrote the whole plan.","If a student wants to speak, they should sign up, and then you get five minutes.","The committee finished their report on Thursday.","Between you and I, the gym was far too hot."],key:["The council gave the microphone to Dani and me. The pronoun is the object of the preposition to, so it takes the objective case. Testing it alone settles it: nobody would say “gave it to I.” (L.6.1a and L.6.1b, pronoun case)","We sixth graders wrote the whole plan. The pronoun is the subject, so it takes the subjective case. (L.6.1a)","If a student wants to speak, they should sign up, and then they get five minutes. The sentence shifts from third person to second person partway through. Accept a version that uses he or she throughout instead. (L.6.1c, shift in person)","The committee finished its report on Thursday. Committee is singular here, so the pronoun has to be singular too. (L.6.1c, shift in number)","Between you and me, the gym was far too hot. Between is a preposition, so both pronouns after it are objects. (L.6.1a and L.6.1e, a common usage error)"]},{title:"Name the Part",std:["L.6.1"],dur:40,dir:"Answer out loud. Say what the word is doing, not only what it is.",desc:"intensive pronouns, vague pronoun reference, and recognizing variations from standard English",qs:["Name the intensive pronoun and say what it does: The principal herself carried the chairs out.","Fix the vague pronoun: Dani told Mireille that her slide was missing.","Which is right, and why? A: Who wrote the report? B: Whom wrote the report?","The column says “Call it what you like.” Is that a complete sentence? What kind is it?"],key:["herself. An intensive pronoun adds emphasis to the noun beside it, here that the principal and nobody else carried them. The sentence still works with it removed, which is what tells you it is intensive rather than reflexive. (L.6.1b, intensive pronouns)","Any version that makes the owner clear, such as: Dani told Mireille that Mireille's slide was missing, or Dani said to Mireille, “My slide is missing.” As written, her could be either girl. (L.6.1d, correcting vague pronouns)","A. Who is the subject of wrote, so it takes the subjective case. Whom is for objects. (L.6.1a, pronoun case)","Yes, it is complete. It is an imperative sentence: the subject you is understood, and the verb call gives the instruction. (L.6.1e, recognizing variations from standard English and knowing when they are correct)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Read the Committee",short:"Listen",parts:[{std:["SL.6.2"],n:5,dur:110,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads the committee's report. Then use the chart to answer. Say what the chart adds that the report did not.",desc:"interpreting information presented in two formats, a report read aloud and a survey chart, and explaining what each contributes",read:COUNCIL_READ,html:()=>lead("Your teacher will read you the student council committee's report. Listen for what it recommends and for the caution at the end. Then look at the chart of the survey it mentions."),fig:()=>F.row([surveyChart(),"The survey the committee ran"]),qs:["What does the committee recommend? Say it in your own words.","What does the chart show that the spoken report did not?","The committee's recommendation keeps a short gathering. Does the chart support that part or work against it? Explain using the numbers.","What caution did the report give about the survey? Why does that caution matter when you read the chart?","Together, what do the report and the chart tell you that neither gives you on its own?"],key:["Split the day: keep a gathering of no more than twenty minutes for awards and thank-yous, move the slideshow and anything that has to be seen or heard in detail to the hallway screens, and give the rest of the time to the field. (SL.6.2, interpreting a text read aloud)","The actual numbers behind each choice: 72 for field games, 50 for a half day, 44 for the gym assembly, and 14 with no opinion. The report mentioned a survey of 180 sixth graders but gave none of the counts. (SL.6.2, information presented quantitatively)","It works partly against it and partly for it. Only 44 of the 180 picked the gym assembly, which is the smallest real choice, so the chart gives no mandate for keeping a gathering. But 44 is still about a quarter of those asked, and 50 wanted to go home early rather than do anything, so the chart does not show a majority against a short gathering either. (SL.6.2, relating a chart to a claim)","That only sixth graders were asked: no seventh grade, no eighth grade, no staff. It matters because the chart's title and totals cover 180 students, and it would be easy to read the bars as what the school wants rather than what one grade wants. (SL.6.2, a caution that changes how the data is read)","The report gives the reasoning and the limits; the chart gives the size of each preference. Only together do you learn both that the field was the most popular single choice and that the committee knows the survey cannot speak for the whole school. Either one alone would mislead. (SL.6.2, how each format contributes)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Source Desk",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.6.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:115,dir:"Read both sources. Answer numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Write numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"assessing the credibility of sources, choosing search terms, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism, and citing",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<ol class="qlist"><li>Which source would you trust on this question? Give two reasons drawn from the source information itself.</li><li>You want one more source. Which search terms are best, and why? A: gym. B: reverberation time school gymnasium speech intelligibility audience. C: best bluetooth speakers.</li><li>Spot the plagiarism. A student report says, with no quotation marks and no source named: <em>Adding an audience of 300 or more brought the average down to 1.3 seconds.</em> Fix it on your paper. Quote it properly, or paraphrase it and name the author.</li><li>Write a citation for Source A with the author, title, publisher and year.</li></ol>`,key:["Source A. Two reasons drawn from the source lines, such as: it names an author with relevant expertise, it comes from a named laboratory, it is dated, and it reports measurements from fourteen sites rather than one opinion. Source B is an undated message-board comment from an anonymous user who writes “Honestly I think” and gives no evidence. For the teacher: Source B is also wrong on the facts, since turning a speaker up raises the bounced sound as much as the direct sound, so it does not improve clarity. (W.6.8, assessing credibility)","B. It uses the technical words for the actual question, so the results will be about how speech is understood in a gym rather than about gyms or speakers in general. A is far too broad and C is a shopping search. (W.6.8, search terms)","Either fix earns the check. Quote: Dr. Priyanka Raval reports that “adding an audience of 300 or more brought the average down to 1.3 seconds.” Paraphrase: according to Dr. Priyanka Raval, a crowd of three hundred or more cut the average reverberation time in the gyms she measured to about 1.3 seconds. The source must be named either way, and a quotation must carry quotation marks. (W.6.8, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism)","All four parts present. Example: Raval, Priyanka. “Speech Clarity in School Multi-Purpose Rooms: Measurements at 14 Sites.” Westmoor Acoustics Laboratory, 2024. Any clear and consistent order is fine. (W.6.8, basic bibliographic information)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Take It to the Council",short:"Present",parts:[{std:["SL.6.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:110,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then speak for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"presenting a claim and findings with pertinent facts and details, in a logical order, with eye contact, volume, and clear pronunciation",html:()=>lead("The student council has asked you to recommend what Linden Park should do on the last day. Use the survey and what you know about the gym.")+surveyTable()+factBox("Also on the record",["Last June's assembly was scheduled for one hour and ran twenty-five minutes over.","The sound system was out for about ten minutes, and about 400 students were in the room.","The sixth-grade slideshow was cut that day and later ran on the hallway screens for nine days.","A full gym has a reverberation time of about 2 seconds, against under 0.6 seconds for a classroom.","The survey asked sixth graders only."])+lead("Give your claim, your two strongest findings, the weakness in your own case, and what you want the council to do.")+pills("My recommendation","Finding 1","Finding 2","The weakness","What I need from the council"),key:["States a clear claim up front and keeps every later point tied to it. Any recommendation can earn the check. (SL.6.4, presenting a claim)","Sequences ideas logically rather than reading the table out row by row (SL.6.4)","Uses pertinent facts and details that actually bear on the claim, with the numbers used accurately: 72 of 180 for the field, 44 for the gym, twenty-five minutes over, ten minutes of lost sound, nine days on the screens (SL.6.4)","Names the weakness in the student's own case, such as that the survey covered sixth graders only, or that awards really do need everyone in one room, or that the field plan depends on the weather (SL.6.4)","Appropriate eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, and clear pronunciation for the whole 1 to 2 minutes (SL.6.4)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Writing Desk",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"explain",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"Why a gym swallows a voice"},std:["W.6.2"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:190,dir:"Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"an informative writing piece built from an article",html:()=>lead("Write to explain to a younger student why a voice is hard to hear in a gym, and what makes it better. Use the article. Write 8 to 10 sentences.")+checklist(["Introduce the topic and group related information together","Develop it with facts, definitions and concrete details from the article","Use transitions to show how your ideas connect: because, as a result, however, for example","Use precise language and the technical terms the article gives you, and explain any term you use","Keep a formal style","End with a conclusion that follows from the information you gave"])+`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at the article</summary>${gym()}</details>`,key:["Introduces the topic and groups related information in a way that helps the reader (W.6.2a)","Develops the topic with relevant facts, definitions and concrete details from the article, such as hard surfaces, bounced sound, buried consonants, 0.6 seconds against 2 seconds (W.6.2b)","Uses appropriate transitions to clarify the relationships among ideas (W.6.2c)","Uses precise language and domain-specific vocabulary, such as reverberation time, absorb, consonant, and explains any term a younger reader would not know (W.6.2d)","Maintains a formal style (W.6.2e)","Provides a concluding statement that follows from the information presented (W.6.2f)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"✨",hint:"A plan that did not survive the day"},std:["W.6.3"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:190,dir:"Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>lead("Write the story of something you had planned carefully that did not go the way you planned on the day, and what came of it afterwards. It can be real or invented. Write 10 to 12 sentences.")+pills("At first","Then","After that","Later")+checklist(["Set up the situation, the narrator and the point of view, and introduce the people in it","Tell the events in an order the reader can follow","Use dialogue, description, or pacing to develop the events and the narrator's response","Use transition words and phrases that show shifts in time and setting","Use precise words and sensory details","End in a way that follows from and reflects on what happened"]),key:["Engages the reader by establishing a context and a point of view and introducing a narrator and characters (W.6.3a)","Organizes an event sequence that unfolds naturally and logically (W.6.3a)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, pacing and description to develop events and characters (W.6.3b)","Uses a variety of transition words, phrases and clauses to convey sequence and signal shifts (W.6.3c)","Uses precise words and phrases, relevant descriptive details and sensory language (W.6.3d)","Provides a conclusion that follows from and reflects on the narrated events (W.6.3e)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"argue",label:"Argue",icon:"⚖️",hint:"Gym, field, or half day?"},std:["W.6.1"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:190,dir:"Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"an argument writing piece built from a fact box",html:()=>lead("Linden Park has to choose one way to mark the last day. Write an argument for the one it should choose. Use the fact box. Write 8 to 10 sentences.")+factBox("Fact box: what is on the record",["Survey of all 180 sixth graders: field games 72, half day 50, gym assembly 44, no opinion 14.","The survey asked sixth graders only. The seventh grade, the eighth grade and the staff were not asked.","Last June's assembly was scheduled for one hour and ran twenty-five minutes over, with about 400 students in the gym.","The sound system was out for about ten minutes and the sixth-grade slideshow was cut.","The slideshow later ran on the hallway screens for nine days and reached more people than the gym holds.","A full gym has a reverberation time of about 2 seconds. A classroom aims for under 0.6 seconds.","Awards and thank-yous are the one part that needs everyone in one room at one time.","The field plan depends on the weather, and there is no indoor backup that is not the gym."])+checklist(["Introduce your claim and organize your reasons and evidence clearly","Support the claim with clear reasons and relevant evidence from the fact box","Use words, phrases and clauses that show how your claim, reasons and evidence connect","Name the strongest objection to your claim and answer it","Keep a formal style","End with a conclusion that follows from and supports your argument"]),key:["Introduces the claim and organizes the reasons and evidence clearly (W.6.1a)","Supports the claim with clear reasons and relevant evidence from the fact box, with the numbers used accurately (W.6.1b)","Uses words, phrases and clauses to clarify the relationships among the claim, the reasons and the evidence (W.6.1c)","Acknowledges the strongest alternate claim and answers it, such as the survey covering only one grade, or the awards needing one room, or the weather risk on the field (W.6.1a)","Establishes and maintains a formal style (W.6.1d)","Provides a concluding statement that follows from and supports the argument presented (W.6.1e)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Reading the Survey",short:"Ratios",parts:[{std:["6.RP.A.3"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Use the survey table. Work on your paper and say each answer out loud.",desc:"ratio, percent and rate reasoning from a survey table",html:()=>surveyTable(),qs:["Write the ratio of students who chose field games to students who chose the gym assembly, in simplest form.","What percent of the 180 chose field games?","A neighboring school has 450 sixth graders. At the same rate, how many would choose field games?"],key:["Say: Write the ratio of students who chose field games to students who chose the gym assembly, in simplest form. (72 to 44, which simplifies to 18 to 11. Both divide by 4.) Or: divide by 2 twice instead of by 4. Partial: 36 to 22 is one step short of simplest form and is approaching.","Say: What percent of the 180 chose field games? (40 percent. 72 divided by 180 is 0.4.) Or: simplify 72/180 to 2/5, and 2/5 is 40 percent.","Say: A neighboring school has 450 sixth graders. At the same rate, how many would choose field games? (180. 40 percent of 450, or 450 divided by 180 is 2.5 and 72 x 2.5 = 180.) Partial: the scale factor 2.5 found with no final count is approaching.","Rate on the reasoning as well as the number. A tape diagram, a table of equivalent ratios, or scaling the rate all count."]}]},{id:15,title:"Cutting the Cloth",short:"Divide",parts:[{std:["6.NS.A.1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Work on your paper. Show the division and hold it up.",desc:"dividing a fraction by a fraction, in a word problem and as a computation",html:()=>lead("The banner crew cuts flags out of a roll of cloth.")+`<ol class="qlist" style="font-size:1.4rem"><li>The roll holds 3/4 of a yard. One flag takes 1/8 of a yard. How many flags come out of the roll?</li><li>Compute (2/3) &divide; (3/4).</li></ol>`,key:["6 flags. 3/4 divided by 1/8 = 3/4 x 8/1 = 24/4 = 6. The question is how many eighths fit in three quarters. Or: common denominators, 6/8 ÷ 1/8 = 6 ÷ 1 = 6.","8/9. 2/3 x 4/3 = 8/9. Or: common denominators, 8/12 ÷ 9/12 = 8 ÷ 9 = 8/9.","Say: How do you know 8/9 is right, using multiplication? (Because 3/4 of 8/9 is 2/3. Multiplying the quotient by the divisor gives the dividend back.)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Over and Under",short:"Signed",parts:[{title:"Above and Below Zero",std:["6.NS.C.5"],n:2,dur:50,dir:"Answer out loud. Say what zero means each time.",desc:"using positive and negative numbers for opposite directions and explaining what 0 means",html:()=>lead("Last June the assembly was scheduled for 60 minutes and ran 25 minutes over. Call running over positive."),key:["Say: What number stands for an assembly that finishes 10 minutes early, and what does 0 mean here? (-10, and 0 means the assembly finished exactly on schedule, at 60 minutes.) Partial: −10 with no account of what 0 stands for is approaching.","Say: The field-games fund ended the year 40 dollars short. Write that as a number, and say what 0 would mean for the fund. (-40, and 0 would mean the fund came out exactly even, with nothing left over and nothing owed.) Partial: −40 with no account of what 0 would mean is approaching.","The sign is only half the answer. Rate on whether the student can say what the zero point is in each situation."]},{title:"Points and Opposites",std:["6.NS.C.6"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look at the grid. Answer out loud.",desc:"rational numbers as points on a number line and in the coordinate plane, and opposites",fig:()=>F.row(F.coordGrid({quad4:true,max:6,size:340,points:[[-4,3,"P"]]})),qs:["On a number line, where does -2.5 sit compared with -3?","What is the opposite of -7? What is the opposite of the opposite of 5?","Point P is at"],key:["Say: On a number line, where does -2.5 sit compared with -3? (To the right of -3, halfway between -3 and -2. Further right means greater, so -2.5 is greater than -3.) Partial: the position right with no comparison of which is greater is approaching.","Say: What is the opposite of -7? What is the opposite of the opposite of 5? (7, and 5. Taking the opposite twice returns the number you started with.) Partial: one of the two answers right is approaching.","Say: Point P is at (-4, 3). Which quadrant is it in, and where would it land if you reflected it across the y-axis? (The second quadrant, and the reflection lands at (4, 3). Reflecting across the y-axis changes the sign of the x-coordinate only.) Partial: the quadrant right with the reflection wrong, or the reverse, is approaching."]}]},{id:17,title:"Powers and Formulas",short:"Exponents",parts:[{title:"Whole Number Exponents",std:["6.EE.A.1"],n:2,dur:45,dir:"Work on your paper. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"writing and evaluating numerical expressions with whole-number exponents",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist" style="font-size:1.4rem"><li>Write 2 &times; 2 &times; 2 &times; 2 &times; 2 using an exponent, then find its value.</li><li>Evaluate 5<sup>3</sup> &minus; 4<sup>2</sup>.</li></ol>`,key:["2 to the fifth power, written 2^5, and its value is 32.","109. 5^3 is 125 and 4^2 is 16, and 125 - 16 = 109. Watch for 5 x 3 = 15, which is the common slip. Partial: one of the two powers right is approaching."]},{title:"Put the Number In",std:["6.EE.A.2c"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Work on your paper. Say each answer and its unit out loud.",desc:"evaluating expressions at given values, including formulas with exponents, in the conventional order",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist" style="font-size:1.4rem"><li>Evaluate 3n<sup>2</sup> + 4 when n = 5.</li><li>A prop cube has edge s = 1/2 foot. Use V = s<sup>3</sup>.</li><li>Use A = 6s<sup>2</sup> for the same cube.</li></ol>`,key:["79. The exponent comes before the multiplication: 5^2 is 25, then 3 x 25 is 75, then 75 + 4 is 79. A student who computes (3 x 5)^2 gets 225 and has the order wrong.","1/8 of a cubic foot. (1/2)^3 = 1/8. The unit is cubic feet.","1 1/2 square feet. (1/2)^2 = 1/4, and 6 x 1/4 = 6/4 = 3/2. The unit is square feet, not cubic feet. Or: count six faces of 1/4 square foot each."]}]},{id:18,title:"The Screen on a Loop",short:"Two Variables",parts:[{title:"One Depends on the Other",std:["6.EE.C.9"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Work on your paper. Say your equation out loud.",desc:"writing an equation for two related quantities and reading it as a table",html:()=>lead("The hallway screen plays the 118-photograph slideshow on a loop. One full loop takes 6 minutes."),qs:["Let n be the number of loops and m be the number of minutes. Which is the independent variable and which is the dependent one, and what is the equation?","Make a table for n = 1, 2, 3 and 4.","The screen runs for 7 hours a day. How many complete loops is that?"],key:["Say: Let n be the number of loops and m be the number of minutes. Which is the independent variable and which is the dependent one, and what is the equation? (n, the number of loops, is independent; m, the minutes, depends on it. The equation is m = 6n.) Or: m = n × 6 or 6n = m count equally. Partial: the variables named with no equation, or the equation with no reason, is approaching.","Say: Make a table for n = 1, 2, 3 and 4. (6, 12, 18 and 24 minutes. Each extra loop adds 6 minutes, which is what the 6 in the equation is.) Partial: three of the four values right is approaching.","Say: The screen runs for 7 hours a day. How many complete loops is that? (70. 7 hours is 420 minutes, and 420 divided by 6 is 70.) Or: 10 loops take an hour, so 7 hours take 70. Partial: 420 minutes found with no division is approaching."]},{title:"Area of the Zone",std:["6.G.A.1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look at the zone. Work on your paper. Say each answer with its unit.",desc:"finding areas of triangles, trapezoids and an L shaped polygon by composing and decomposing",fig:()=>F.row([lZoneFig(),"The field-games zone"]),qs:["What is the area of the L shaped zone? Tell me how you cut it up.","A triangular banner has a base of 9 feet and a height of 4 feet. What is its area?","A trapezoid banner has parallel sides of 10 feet and 6 feet and a height of 4 feet. What is its area?"],key:["Say: What is the area of the L shaped zone? Tell me how you cut it up. (210 square feet. Either 20 x 12 = 240 with the 6 x 5 = 30 notch taken off, or 14 x 12 = 168 plus 6 x 7 = 42. Both give 210.)","Say: A triangular banner has a base of 9 feet and a height of 4 feet. What is its area? (18 square feet. 1/2 x 9 x 4 = 18.) Or: 9 × 4 = 36, then halve. Partial: 36 square feet is the whole rectangle and does not count.","Say: A trapezoid banner has parallel sides of 10 feet and 6 feet and a height of 4 feet. What is its area? (32 square feet. Average the parallel sides, (10 + 6) divided by 2 = 8, then 8 x 4 = 32. Cutting it into a rectangle and two triangles gives the same answer.) Partial: the rectangle part alone, 24, is approaching."]}]},{id:19,title:"From Cell to Whole Plant",short:"Cells",parts:[{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the organization of living things and how structures carry out functions",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class grew a bean plant from seed to flower over the year and kept a record of it.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("leaf",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name the levels of organization from a single cell up to the whole plant, and give a plant example at each level.","Choose two structures on this plant and explain how the shape of each one suits what it does.","The plant started as one cell and ended with many kinds of cells. Explain how one set of instructions produces different kinds of cells."],key:["Cell, tissue, organ, organism. For example a leaf cell, the tissue inside the leaf, the leaf or root as an organ, and the whole plant as the organism. Accept organ system if the student names the root or shoot system. Partial: the levels in the right order with an example missing or wrong for one of them is approaching. (LS1)","Any two with a real link: broad flat leaves give a large surface for catching light; fine branching roots give a large surface for taking up water; a hollow stiff stem supports height with little material; flower petals are colored to attract pollinators. Partial: two structures named with the shape linked to the job for only one of them is approaching. (LS1)","Cells specialize. They all carry the same instructions, but different parts of those instructions are used in different cells, so each type develops the structures its job needs. Partial: naming specialization with nothing said about the same instructions being used differently in different cells is approaching. (LS1)"]},{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting a year of growth data and tracing matter and energy through a plant",html:()=>`<p class="big">The class weighed the seed, then weighed the whole dried plant at the end of the year, and recorded conditions.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Value</th></tr>
     <tr><td>mass of the seed</td><td>0.4 g</td></tr>
     <tr><td>dried mass of the whole plant</td><td>61 g</td></tr>
     <tr><td>mass of soil lost from the pot</td><td>about 2 g</td></tr>
     <tr><td>water given over the year</td><td>many liters</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["The plant gained about 60 grams and the soil lost only 2. Where did nearly all the new material come from?","The plant was watered with many liters and gained only 61 grams. Where did the rest of the water go?","Explain where the energy stored in this plant came from and how it got there."],key:["From carbon dioxide taken in through the leaves and from water, built into sugars and then into plant tissue. Very little came from the soil, which supplies minerals rather than bulk. Partial: answering that the material came from the soil is the misconception the 2 gram figure is there to test and is approaching only if the answer also names air or water. (LS1)","Most of it was released as vapor from the leaves through transpiration, some evaporated from the soil, and only a small amount was built into the plant. Partial: saying the water evaporated, with nothing said about most of it leaving through the leaves, is approaching. (LS1)","From sunlight. Chloroplasts in the leaf cells captured light energy and used it to build sugar from carbon dioxide and water, which stores that energy in chemical form. Partial: naming sunlight with nothing said about the energy being stored in sugar built from carbon dioxide and water is approaching. (LS1)"]}]},{id:20,title:"What Gets Passed On",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing inheritance data across two generations",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class tracked seed coat color across two generations of beans.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Cross</th><th>Result</th></tr>
     <tr><td>first</td><td>speckled x plain</td><td>all 18 speckled</td></tr>
     <tr><td>second</td><td>two first generation plants</td><td>29 speckled, 9 plain</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("herb",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why were all the first generation seeds speckled although one parent was plain?","Plain seeds came back in the second generation. Explain how, using the gene versions each plant carries.","The second generation is close to three speckled for every one plain. Explain why it is not exactly three to one."],key:["Speckled is the version that is expressed when a plant carries one of each, so every first generation plant showed speckled while still carrying the plain version. Partial: saying speckled is stronger, with nothing said about the plants still carrying the plain version, is approaching. (LS3)","Each first generation plant carried one speckled and one plain version. When two were crossed, some offspring received the plain version from both parents, so plain was expressed again. Partial: saying the plain version was still being carried, with nothing said about an offspring receiving it from both parents, is approaching. (LS3)","Which version an offspring receives is a matter of chance, so the ratio is a probability. Small samples vary around it and larger samples come closer to it. Partial: saying the numbers will not come out exact, with nothing said about three to one being a probability, is approaching. (LS3)"]},{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"distinguishing inherited from acquired traits and explaining the role of variation",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four facts from the class record: one plant’s leaves are notched. Its offspring’s are too; one plant is bent from leaning toward a window; one plant grew taller after being moved to a bigger pot; one plant has a single white flower among purple ones.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("leaf",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}</div>`,qs:["Sort the four facts into inherited and not inherited, and give your reason for each.","A single white flower appears where no plant had one. Name the kind of change that can do that and say where in the cell it happens.","Explain why a population with many trait differences is better placed to survive a change in conditions than a population of identical individuals."],key:["Inherited: the notched leaves, shown by the offspring having them, and probably the white flower, which should be checked in the next generation. Not inherited: the bend toward the window and the extra height from a bigger pot, both caused by conditions during life. Partial: three of the four sorted correctly is approaching, and the white flower is usually the one placed without a reason. (LS3)","A mutation, a change in a gene, which happens in the genetic material on the chromosomes inside the nucleus. If it is in the cells that make offspring, it can be inherited. Partial: naming the mutation with nothing said about where in the cell the genetic material sits is approaching. (LS3)","Different individuals respond differently to a change, so some are likely to survive and reproduce. In an identical population every individual meets the change with the same traits, so a change that defeats one defeats all. Partial: saying variety is safer, with nothing said about an identical population meeting a change with the same traits, is approaching. (LS3)"]}]},{id:21,title:"How Earth Systems Meet",short:"Systems",parts:[{std:["ESS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how Earth's water, air, rock and living systems interact",html:()=>`<p class="big">Rain falls on a mountain, runs down to a river, crosses farmland, and reaches the sea. Along the way it wears away rock, feeds plants, and evaporates back into the air.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("wave",74)}${ico("cloud",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name one way water changes rock, one way water affects living things, and one way living things affect water.","Trace the whole water cycle in this description, naming each process in order.","Name the two sources of energy that drive the processes changing Earth’s surface, and give one example for each."],key:["Water changes rock by weathering and erosion, breaking and carrying it away. Water affects living things by supplying what plants and animals need to live. Living things affect water by taking it up and releasing vapor, by holding soil so less runs off, and by shading and cooling streams. Partial: two of the three links given is approaching, and living things affecting water is usually the missing one. (ESS2)","Evaporation from the sea and land, condensation into cloud, precipitation as rain, runoff into river and infiltration into the ground, uptake and transpiration by plants, and back to evaporation. Partial: evaporation, condensation and rain named with the runoff and infiltration left out is approaching. (ESS2)","The sun, which drives evaporation, weather, winds and currents, and Earth’s internal energy, which drives plate movement, uplift and volcanic activity. Gravity then moves material downhill. Partial: naming the sun alone, with nothing said about Earth's internal energy, is approaching, and gravity on its own is not one of the two sources. (ESS2)"]},{std:["ESS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting evidence of Earth's changing surface and reading rock layers",html:()=>`<p class="big">A road cut through a hillside shows these layers, top to bottom.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it is made of</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1, top</td><td>loose soil with roots</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>sandstone with ripple marks</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>shale with shells of sea animals</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4, bottom</td><td>sandstone, tilted at an angle</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("shell",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which layer formed first, and what rule tells you?","Layer 3 holds shells of sea animals and sits on a hillside well above the sea. What does that tell you happened here?","Layer 4 is tilted and the layers above it are flat. What does that tell you about the order of events?"],key:["Layer 4, the bottom. Layers are laid down one on top of another, so the lowest undisturbed layer is the oldest. Partial: naming layer 4 with no rule stated about the lowest undisturbed layer being the oldest is approaching. (ESS2)","This place was once under the sea, and sediment with sea animals in it settled here. Later the land was uplifted, or sea level fell, so rock that formed in the ocean now sits on a hillside. Partial: saying the place used to be under the sea, with nothing said about the land being lifted or the sea falling since, is approaching. (ESS2)","Layer 4 was laid down flat, then tilted by movement of the crust, and only afterward were the flat layers deposited on top of it. The tilting happened in between. Partial: saying layer 4 was tilted, with nothing said about the tilting happening before the flat layers were laid on top, is approaching. (ESS2)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Deciding for the Future",short:"Choices",parts:[{std:["ESS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing energy resources and the effects of each choice",html:()=>`<p class="big">A town compared three plans for new electricity.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Renewable?</th><th>Cost to build</th><th>Cost to run</th><th>Main effect</th></tr>
     <tr><td>gas plant</td><td>no</td><td>medium</td><td>fuel every year</td><td>emissions from burning fuel</td></tr>
     <tr><td>wind farm</td><td>yes</td><td>high</td><td>low</td><td>land use, noise, bird strikes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>solar field with batteries</td><td>yes</td><td>high</td><td>low</td><td>land use, battery materials</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wind",74)}${ico("sun",74)}${ico("fire",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which plan is nonrenewable, and what does nonrenewable mean about where the fuel came from?","Every plan in the table has a drawback. What does that tell you about choosing an energy source?","Name two pieces of information the town would need before deciding, that this table does not give."],key:["The gas plant. Its fuel formed from buried organisms over millions of years and is not replaced as it is used. Partial: naming the gas plant with nothing said about the fuel forming over millions of years and not being replaced is approaching. (ESS3)","There is no cost free option, so a decision means weighing different kinds of cost against each other, including who bears each one and for how long. Partial: saying every plan has a problem, with nothing said about a decision meaning weighing one kind of cost against another, is approaching. (ESS3)","Any two: how much wind or sun the area actually gets, how much electricity is needed and when, how long each plant lasts, what happens at the end of its life, who lives nearby, and the total cost over thirty years. Partial: one piece of missing information named where two were asked for is approaching. (ESS3)"]},{std:["ESS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"using long term data to explain a human effect on Earth's systems",html:()=>`<p class="big">A regional record goes back more than a century. It holds average temperatures and the date the local lake freezes each winter. It holds the amount of carbon dioxide in the air.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("thermometer",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}${ico("globe",74)}</div>`,qs:["What could a century of freeze dates show that ten years of them cannot?","Average temperature and carbon dioxide both rose over the record. Why is rising together not by itself proof that one caused the other?","Name two effects on this region that people might plan for if the trend continues."],key:["A long term trend and how much normal year to year variation there is, so an unusual year can be told from a change in the pattern. Ten years can be entirely within normal swings. Partial: saying a century gives more data, with nothing said about telling an unusual year from a change in the pattern, is approaching. (ESS3)","Two things rising together can be coincidence or can both be driven by something else. Showing cause needs a mechanism and further evidence, which in this case is the way carbon dioxide traps outgoing energy, tested separately. Partial: saying it could be a coincidence, with nothing said about cause needing a mechanism and separate evidence, is approaching. (ESS3)","Any two reasonable: a shorter ice season and the effects on lake life, earlier snowmelt and a changed water supply, hotter summers and more water demand, changed growing seasons for crops, and more intense storms. Partial: one effect named where two were asked for is approaching. (ESS3)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Energy End to End",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["PS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud. You may use your paper.",desc:"tracing energy transformations and relating kinetic energy to mass and speed",html:()=>`<p class="big">A student rides a bicycle to the top of a hill, then coasts down the other side and brakes to a stop at the bottom.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("bike",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("heart",74)}</div>`,qs:["Follow the energy from the student’s breakfast to the moment the bicycle stops, naming each form in order.","At the bottom of the hill, a heavier rider and a lighter rider reach the same speed. Which has more kinetic energy, and why?","After braking hard, the brake pads are hot. Explain why in terms of energy."],key:["Chemical energy stored in food is released in the student’s cells, transferred to kinetic energy of the legs and bicycle, stored as gravitational potential energy climbing the hill, converted back to kinetic energy coasting down, and transferred away by the brakes as thermal energy and sound. Partial: three or four of the forms named in order with one of the steps missing is approaching, and the store as potential energy on the climb is usually the one left out. (PS3)","The heavier rider, because kinetic energy depends on mass as well as speed, and both are at the same speed. Partial: choosing the heavier rider with nothing said about both being at the same speed is approaching. (PS3)","Braking transfers the kinetic energy of the bicycle and rider out of their motion by friction, and most of it ends up as thermal energy in the pads, the wheel rims and the air. The energy was moved, not destroyed. Partial: naming friction in the brakes with nothing said about the kinetic energy ending up as thermal energy is approaching. (PS3)"]},{std:["PS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting data on thermal energy transfer and what a temperature change depends on",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three containers were left on the same table in a warm room and measured.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Container</th><th>Start</th><th>After thirty minutes</th></tr>
     <tr><td>100 mL of ice water in glass</td><td>2 °C</td><td>14 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>500 mL of ice water in glass</td><td>2 °C</td><td>7 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>100 mL of ice water in a vacuum flask</td><td>2 °C</td><td>3 °C</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("thermometer",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("cocoa",74)}</div>`,qs:["Both glasses started at 2 degrees in the same room. Explain why the small one warmed more.","The vacuum flask barely changed. Explain how a vacuum flask slows energy transfer.","Energy moved into all three containers. Say which direction energy always moves and why these three will all end at room temperature."],key:["The same amount of energy flowing in raises the temperature of a small mass more than a large one, so 100 mL warmed 12 degrees while 500 mL warmed only 5. Temperature change depends on mass as well as on energy. Partial: saying the small glass has less water in it, with nothing said about temperature change depending on mass, is approaching. (PS3)","The gap between its walls has almost no matter in it, so there is very little to conduct energy or to circulate, and the shiny walls reflect radiation back. All three transfer paths are cut down at once. Partial: naming the vacuum with nothing said about the shiny walls reflecting radiation back is approaching. (PS3)","Energy always moves from warmer to cooler. The room is warmer than all three, so energy flows in until each one matches the room, which is thermal equilibrium. The flask only makes it slower, not impossible. Partial: naming the direction with nothing said about the flask slowing the transfer rather than stopping it is approaching. (PS3)"]}]},{id:24,title:"The Whole Design Cycle",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud. Write your criteria and constraints on your paper.",desc:"defining a design problem and planning a fair test",html:()=>`<p class="big">The class wants a banner for the end of year display that will hang outside above the doors. Available: cloth, cord, light poles, grommets, paint, and two class periods. It must be readable from the car park, must survive a week of wind and rain, and must come down without a ladder.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("flag",74)}${ico("wind",74)}${ico("rain",74)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence that names the need rather than a design.","Write two criteria that could be measured and two constraints from the description.","Describe a fair test for the readable from the car park criterion."],key:["Something like: display a readable banner outside above the doors for a week of weather. Partial: a sentence that names cloth or grommets rather than the result to be achieved is approaching. (ETS1)","Criteria: readable from a stated distance, still hanging and legible after a week, goes up in two class periods. Constraints: only the listed materials, two class periods, must come down without a ladder, must handle wind and rain. Partial: two criteria and one constraint, or one of each, is approaching. (ETS1)","Hang it, then have several people stand at a marked spot in the car park and read it aloud, in ordinary daylight, without being told what it says. Success is everyone reading it correctly from that distance. Partial: a test described with nothing said about the readers not being told in advance what the banner says is approaching. (ETS1)"]},{std:["ETS1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing tested solutions, combining the best features and explaining iteration",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three banners were tested for a week in the same spot.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Banner</th><th>Readable from the car park</th><th>After a week of weather</th><th>Coming down</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: thin cloth, large letters</td><td>yes</td><td>torn at the grommets</td><td>easy, cord to the rail</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: heavy cloth, small letters</td><td>no</td><td>no damage</td><td>easy, cord to the rail</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: heavy cloth, large letters, nailed up</td><td>yes</td><td>no damage</td><td>needs a ladder</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("flag",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("board",74)}</div>`,qs:["No banner meets everything. Name what each one got right and what each one got wrong.","Describe the banner you would build next, taking the best feature from each result.","Explain why designers test, change and test again rather than building the final version first."],key:["A: readable and easy to take down, but it tore. B: durable and easy to take down, but not readable. C: readable and durable, but it needs a ladder, which breaks a constraint. Partial: a right and a wrong given for two of the three banners is approaching. (ETS1)","Heavy cloth for durability, large letters for readability, reinforced grommets, hung with cord tied off at the rail so it comes down without a ladder. That takes one strength from each test. Partial: a next banner described that still needs a ladder, which breaks a constraint, is not yet there; a design that takes two of the three strengths is approaching. (ETS1)","Because a test shows what the design actually does rather than what it was expected to do, and each round turns up problems that could not be predicted, such as tearing at the grommets. Changing one thing at a time also shows which change helped. Partial: saying testing finds mistakes, with no example named such as the tearing at the grommets, is approaching. (ETS1)"]}]},{id:25,title:"What a Site Celebrates",short:"The Site",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the dig notes. Answer out loud.",desc:"judging what remains of a large gathering can and cannot show about early people",html:()=>`<p class="big">Diggers found an unusual deposit at the edge of an early village: a single pit holding the bones of forty animals, broken pottery of many different shapes, and the remains of one very large fire. Nothing like it appears anywhere else at the site.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("fire",74)}${ico("pan",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["Forty animals, many pots and one huge fire, all in one pit. What kind of event would leave that, and why does the amount matter to your answer?","Nothing else at the site looks like this. What does that tell you about how often such an event happened?","A visitor says the deposit proves these people held a yearly festival. Is that claim supported by the evidence? Explain."],key:["One large gathering where many people ate together, because forty animals is far more than a household would use at once and many pot shapes suggest many families bringing their own. Scale is what separates a feast from ordinary meals. Partial: naming the feast with nothing said about the scale as the evidence is approaching. (HSS-6.1)","That it was rare and special rather than routine, since routine activity leaves repeated traces across a site. One of a kind remains record an unusual occasion. Partial: saying it was special with no contrast to repeated traces is approaching. (HSS-6.1)","No. The evidence supports one large shared meal. Yearly means it repeated on a schedule, and a single deposit cannot show a schedule. The student should say what would be needed: several similar deposits at regular intervals. Partial: saying no with nothing named that would be needed to show a schedule is approaching. (HSS-6.1)"]},{std:["HSS-6.2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing what the monuments of Mesopotamia, Egypt and Kush required of those societies",html:()=>`<p class="big">Mesopotamia built ziggurats of mud brick, stepped towers at the heart of a city. Egypt built pyramids and temples of quarried stone. Kush built its own pyramids at Meroe, steeper and smaller, hundreds of them. All three were raised by large organized workforces over many years.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("sun",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["What does a society need to have in place before it can build a monument that takes years?","Kush built many small steep pyramids and Egypt built a few enormous ones. What might that difference say about each kingdom, and how sure can you be?","These monuments were religious buildings paid for by the state. What does that tell you about how religion and government fit together in these societies?"],key:["A food surplus to feed workers who are not farming, an authority able to organize and direct thousands of people, specialists who can plan and survey, and a steady enough government to carry a project across years. Partial: one requirement named is approaching. (HSS-6.2)","Egypt had the resources and the single central command to concentrate on a few huge projects. Kush spread its building across many rulers and burials. The reading is reasonable but not certain: size also depends on stone, labor and local custom. Partial: stating the reading as certain rather than reasonable is approaching. (HSS-6.2)","They were the same enterprise. The state spent its surplus on religious building, and the ruler’s authority was displayed and explained through it, so religious and political power reinforced each other. Partial: treating religious and political power as separate is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-6.2)"]}]},{id:26,title:"What They Left Us",short:"Legacies",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the lasting contributions of Ancient Hebrew law and belief",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two ideas from Judaism, the religion of the Ancient Hebrews, outlasted their kingdom. One is monotheism, the belief in a single God. The other is that law stands above everyone, including the ruler, and that a ruler who breaks it can be told so. Both were carried in the Torah, their written law.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("board",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why is the idea that law stands above the ruler a large change from the way most ancient kingdoms worked?","The Hebrews kept their identity after losing their kingdom. According to the note, what did they have that made that possible?","Name one way the ideas in this note still show up in how countries are governed today."],key:["In most ancient kingdoms the ruler was the source of law, or was treated as chosen by the gods, so there was nothing above him to appeal to. Law above the ruler means power can be judged by a standard it did not create. Partial: saying the ruler had to obey with no contrast to other kingdoms is approaching. (HSS-6.3)","Judaism itself: the Torah, their written law, plus the belief in one God and a shared history, all portable. Identity rested on those rather than on a territory or a building, so it survived the loss of both. Partial: naming the Torah with nothing said about identity being portable is approaching. (HSS-6.3)","Any reasonable answer: written constitutions, the idea that leaders are subject to the law, courts that can rule against a government, or the expectation that law is public and applies to everyone. Partial: an answer with no link to law standing above a ruler is approaching. (HSS-6.3)"]},{std:["HSS-6.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Greek contributions in government, thought and the arts",html:()=>`<p class="big">Greek city-states left behind more than ruins. They left citizen government and the word democracy itself. They left theater with written plays performed for the whole city. They left philosophy that argued from reasons rather than from custom. They left geometry set out as proofs. They also left the practice of writing history by questioning witnesses.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("trophy",74)}${ico("palette",74)}${ico("books",74)}</div>`,qs:["Athenian democracy was direct and narrow, and ours is representative and far wider. What did Athens contribute, given those differences?","Greek thinkers argued from reasons rather than from custom or authority. Why does that habit matter beyond philosophy?","Pick one item in the note and explain how you meet it in your own life."],key:["The idea itself: that ordinary citizens rather than a king or a few families should decide public questions, plus the working machinery of assemblies, juries and public debate. Later societies widened who counted as a citizen. Partial: naming the idea with nothing said about the machinery, or the reverse, is approaching. (HSS-6.4)","It is the basis of argument and proof: a claim with reasons can be examined, tested and overturned by better reasons, which is how science, law and mathematics work. Custom cannot be checked. Partial: saying reasons are good with no account of a claim being testable is approaching. (HSS-6.4)","Any real connection: voting or class decisions, a play or film, geometry in math class, a court or jury, an argument settled by reasons, or reading history that cites sources. Partial: naming something Greek with no connection to the student's own week is approaching. (HSS-6.4)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Numbers, Paper and Law",short:"Handed Down",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing lasting Indian contributions in mathematics, literature and belief",html:()=>`<p class="big">From early India came the numerals most of the world now writes with, including a symbol and a place for zero, long epic poems still performed and retold, and two of the world’s major religions. Hinduism and Buddhism both began in India. Buddhism spread across much of Asia, carried by traders, travelers and the support of rulers, while Hinduism stayed centered in India and reached southeast Asia by sea.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("calculator",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("globe",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why is a symbol for zero, and a place for it in a number, such a large step for mathematics?","Buddhism began in India and spread across much of Asia. Using the note and what you know about trade, explain how.","Long epic poems were performed and retold for centuries before being written down. What does that tell you about how a society keeps what it values?"],key:["It makes place value work: zero holds an empty column, so 205 is different from 25, and arithmetic with large numbers becomes simple to write and to calculate. Without it, written calculation is far harder. Partial: calling zero useful with no account of holding an empty column is approaching. (HSS-6.5)","Buddhism traveled with traders and travelers along the land and sea routes, carried into port cities and market towns, and was taken up there rather than imposed by force. Rulers who supported it also helped it spread. Trade routes were idea routes. Partial: saying it spread with nothing said about the trade routes is approaching. (HSS-6.5)","That memory, performance and teaching are a real technology for keeping knowledge, and a society will invest enormous effort in holding on to what it considers important even without writing. Partial: saying they had good memories with no account of the effort invested is approaching. (HSS-6.5)"]},{std:["HSS-6.6"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing lasting Chinese contributions in materials, government and writing",html:()=>`<p class="big">China gave the world paper, silk, the compass and printing, and a system in which officials were chosen by examination on the Confucian classics rather than by birth. Confucianism, Daoism and Buddhism all shaped Chinese ideas about government and the family for two thousand years. China’s writing system has been in continuous use longer than any other.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("compass",74)}${ico("pencil",74)}${ico("scarf",74)}</div>`,qs:["Choosing officials by examination on the Confucian classics instead of by birth changes who can govern. Name one effect of that change, and one limit on it.","Paper is cheap, light and easy to make in quantity. Why does a material like that matter to a government and to learning?","A writing system in continuous use for thousands of years does something unusual for a country. What is it?"],key:["Effect: ability and study can raise a person’s standing, so government draws on talent from outside the noble families, and every official has studied the same Confucian texts, which gives government one shared outlook. Limit: only people with the time and money to study for years could prepare, so it still favored the better off, and women were excluded. Partial: one of the effect and the limit given is approaching. (HSS-6.6)","Records, orders and books can be made in quantity and sent everywhere, so a government can administer a huge territory and knowledge can be copied and spread instead of staying with a few. Partial: one of the government and the knowledge effects named is approaching. (HSS-6.6)","It lets people read what was written centuries earlier, so law, history, poetry and records stay directly available rather than needing translation, which ties a country to its own past. Partial: saying the writing lasted with nothing said about reading the past directly is approaching. (HSS-6.6)"]},{std:["HSS-6.7"],n:3,dur:125,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Roman contributions in law, engineering, language and government",html:()=>`<p class="big">Rome left written law codes and the ideas of a republic and of citizenship. It left roads and aqueducts. It left the arch and concrete that made large buildings possible. It left the Latin language, which became Italian, Spanish, French, Portuguese and Romanian. Latin also supplied a great many English words. Rome also passed on Christianity. It began in the Roman province of Judea with the teachings of Jesus of Nazareth. It was outlawed under Roman law for nearly three centuries. Christians who would not give it up were persecuted, losing property and legal standing and in some cases their lives. The emperor Constantine legalized it in 313. Before the end of that century it had become the official religion of the empire. It spread along Rome’s roads and sea lanes.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("bridge",74)}${ico("board",74)}${ico("globe",74)}</div>`,qs:["Rome ran as a republic for centuries before it became an empire. What did later countries take from the republic rather than from the empire? And according to the note, name the three stages Christianity passed through in Roman law.","Concrete and the arch let Romans build larger than before. Why do materials and shapes decide what a society can build?","Latin stopped being anyone’s first language, and yet it is still inside the languages millions of people speak. Explain how that happened."],key:["Elected officials with fixed terms, a senate, written law, the vote, and the idea that power should be divided so no one person holds it all. Many later governments copied the forms and the vocabulary. The three stages: outlawed and persecuted for nearly three centuries, legalized by Constantine in 313, then the official religion of the empire before the end of that century. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the Roman forms with nothing on the three stages sits there. (HSS-6.7)","An arch carries weight outward into its supports instead of straight down, so openings can be wide, and concrete can be poured into any shape and sets hard, so domes and vaults become possible. What you can build limits what you can do with a city. Partial: one of the arch and the concrete explained is approaching. (HSS-6.7)","Latin spread as the language of Roman government and trade across the empire, and in each region ordinary spoken Latin drifted into a local form over centuries, which became the separate languages. English borrowed from those and from Latin directly. Partial: naming Latin with nothing said about it drifting into local forms is approaching. (HSS-6.7)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: Several Pieces of Evidence",std:["RL.7.1"],needs:["RL.6.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite several pieces of evidence for each answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Claim: the story treats the gym, not the power cut, as the real problem. Cite several pieces of evidence and explain what each one adds.</li><li>What does the story suggest about why the slideshow mattered to other students, not only to Octavio? Cite several pieces of evidence.</li></ol>${story()}`,key:["Several pieces, each explained. The gym is hot before anything starts. The propped doors “helped the air and ruined the sound,” so the room forces a trade with no good side. “Nobody past the fourth row heard a word” of the chorus, which happened while the sound system was still working. The ten silent minutes are described as a crowd noise problem rather than a technical one. And the slideshow succeeds in September precisely by leaving the gym. The power cut is one bad morning; the room fails on every point. (RL.7.1, citing several pieces of evidence)","Several pieces: a seventh grader “he barely knew” asked for it, then another one did, which shows interest beyond his own class and beyond people who knew him. The office put it on the hallway screen, so adults judged there was demand. It ran “for nine days,” which is far longer than a single showing would need. And “more people watched it there than would ever have fit in the gym.” Together these show the slideshow mattered as a record of the year to the school generally. (RL.7.1)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Compare Two Groups",std:["7.SP.B.4"],needs:["6.SP.B.5"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Compare the two groups, do not just describe each one.",html:()=>`<p class="big">A seventh-grade teacher timed eleven seventh-grade classes setting up the same stations.</p>${bigTable(["Seventh-grade minutes, in order"],[["14, 16, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 24, 25, 26, 27"]])}<ol class="qlist"><li>Find the median and the interquartile range for the seventh grade.</li><li>Compare the two grades using a measure of center and a measure of variability. Which group was quicker, and which was more consistent?</li><li>The difference between the two medians is small. Use the variability to say whether that difference means much here.</li></ol>${bigTable(["Sixth-grade minutes, in order"],[[SETUP.join(", ")]])}`,key:["Median 21 minutes, the sixth of the eleven values. The lower half is 14, 16, 18, 19, 20, so the first quartile is 18. The upper half is 22, 24, 25, 26, 27, so the third quartile is 25. The interquartile range is 25 − 18 = 7 minutes. (7.SP.B.4, measures of center and variability for a second sample)","The seventh grade was slightly quicker at the center, median 21 against 22, and much more consistent: an interquartile range of 7 minutes against 13, and a full range of 14 to 27 against 12 to 65. The sixth-grade mean, 25, is pulled above its own median by the 65, while the seventh-grade data has no such value. (7.SP.B.4, comparative inference from two samples)","Not much on its own. One minute of difference in the medians is small next to a spread of 7 minutes in one group and 13 in the other, so a single class landing differently would move it. The real difference between the grades is in consistency, not in speed. (7.SP.B.4, informally assessing the difference between two centers against the variability)"]},3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Cite Several",std:["RI.7.1"],needs:["RI.6.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite several pieces of evidence for each answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Claim: a louder speaker would not fix a gym. Use several pieces of evidence from the article to support that claim.</li><li>Why would a gym be hardest for the people sitting furthest from the stage? Cite several pieces of evidence.</li></ol>${gym()}`,key:["Several pieces. The article says the problem is that hard surfaces “send sound back instead of absorbing it,” so a louder source produces louder reflections as well as a louder direct sound. It says the bounced sound “arrives a fraction of a second late, so one voice becomes a smear of overlapping copies,” and nothing about volume changes that timing. It says consonants are lost because they are “quick and quiet” relative to the smear, which raising everything together does not separate. What the article names as the fix is absorption, which is why a crowd helps. (RI.7.1, citing several pieces of evidence)","Several pieces. The direct sound is one part of what reaches the ear and the bounced sound is the rest, so the further away you sit the weaker the direct part is against the reflections. The article's measure of the problem, reverberation time, describes the whole room, and it reports a full gym at around 2 seconds against under 0.6 for a classroom. And the crowd that absorbs sound sits in front of the far seats, not behind them. (RI.7.1)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Wrap It as Well as Fill It",std:["7.G.B.6"],needs:["6.G.A.2"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit in every answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A prop box is 4 ft by 3 ft by 2 ft. Find its volume and its surface area.</li><li>A lid has to cover the open top of that box and fold 1 ft down each of the four sides. How much material does the lid need, in square feet? The corners do not overlap.</li><li>A triangular sign has a base of 9 inches and a height of 6 inches. Find its area. Then find the volume of a prism 5 inches long with that triangle as its base.</li></ol>`,key:["Volume 24 cubic feet, from 4 × 3 × 2. Surface area 52 square feet: the three pairs of faces are 4 × 3 = 12, 4 × 2 = 8 and 3 × 2 = 6, so 2 × (12 + 8 + 6) = 52. (7.G.B.6, volume and surface area)","26 square feet. The top is 4 × 3 = 12. The two long flaps are 4 × 1 = 4 each, for 8, and the two short flaps are 3 × 1 = 3 each, for 6. 12 + 8 + 6 = 26. (7.G.B.6, area of a figure made of rectangles)","The triangle is 27 square inches, from 1/2 × 9 × 6. The prism is 135 cubic inches, from 27 × 5. (7.G.B.6, area of a triangle and the volume of a prism built on it)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Phrases and Clauses",std:["L.7.1"],needs:["L.6.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud, then name the phrase or clause you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Combine these into one sentence using a subordinate clause: “The sound system failed. The chorus had already finished.”</li><li>Fix the misplaced modifier: “Running on a loop for nine days, students watched the slideshow in the hallway.”</li><li>Name the phrase and say what it modifies: “The committee, after eight weeks of meetings, finally voted.”</li></ol>`,key:["Any correct version built on a subordinate clause, such as: “The sound system failed after the chorus had already finished,” or “Although the chorus had already finished, the sound system failed.” The student names the subordinate clause. (L.7.1a and L.7.1b, phrases and clauses)","“Students watched the slideshow, which ran on a loop for nine days, in the hallway,” or “The slideshow, running on a loop for nine days, was watched by students in the hallway.” As written, the modifier attaches to students, so the sentence says the students ran on a loop. (L.7.1c, misplaced modifiers)","“after eight weeks of meetings” is a prepositional phrase, and it modifies the verb voted by telling when. (L.7.1a, the function of phrases in specific sentences)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Weigh the Sources",std:["W.7.8"],needs:["W.6.8"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<ol class="qlist"><li>Name one thing Source A measured and one thing it did not, and say what question its evidence cannot answer.</li><li>Source B makes a claim about physics. Write the claim, then write what you would need to check it.</li><li>Write a sentence for a report that uses Source A's finding, with the source named and the number used accurately, and follows a standard citation format at the end.</li></ol>`,key:["Measured: reverberation time empty and full, across fourteen gyms, and word recognition for listeners beyond row six. Not measured: anything about a louder speaker, any single named school, cost, or whether an audience of fewer than 300 helps. Its evidence cannot answer whether more volume fixes the problem, because volume was never a variable in it. (W.7.8, assessing the usefulness of a source for a specific question)","The claim is that more volume gives more clarity. To check it you would need a measurement of speech intelligibility at the same seats at two speaker volumes in the same room, with everything else held the same, reported by someone who names their method. A message-board assertion with no method and no numbers cannot settle it. (W.7.8, assessing credibility and accuracy)","A sentence that attributes the finding and reports the figure correctly, such as: Raval found that filling a gym with an audience of 300 or more cut the average reverberation time from 2.4 seconds to 1.3 seconds (Raval, 2024). Followed by a full citation: Raval, Priyanka. “Speech Clarity in School Multi-Purpose Rooms: Measurements at 14 Sites.” Westmoor Acoustics Laboratory, 2024. (W.7.8, quoting or paraphrasing with a standard format for citation)"]},13:{id:"L13",title:"Level Up: Tighten the Explanation",std:["W.7.2"],needs:["W.6.2"],n:3,dir:"Add to the writing on your paper, or read the new sentences out loud.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Add three or four sentences to what you just wrote.</p><ol class="qlist"><li>Add a comparison or a contrast that makes one of your facts easier to grasp, and use a transition that names the relationship.</li><li>Define one technical term inside the sentence that uses it, so a reader never has to stop.</li><li>Rewrite your closing so that it follows from the information and does not introduce a new fact.</li></ol>`,key:["Adds a comparison or contrast that genuinely helps, such as setting 2 seconds in a gym against under 0.6 in a classroom, or a carpeted room against a block wall, and marks it with a transition such as by contrast, compared with, or on the other hand. (W.7.2c, transitions that create cohesion and clarify relationships)","Defines a term in place, such as explaining reverberation time as how long a sound takes to fade away, inside the sentence that first uses it, so the reader is not sent elsewhere. (W.7.2d, precise language and domain-specific vocabulary)","The closing follows from the information presented and supports it, and adds no fact that was not already explained. (W.7.2f, conclusion that supports the information presented)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.6.1","RL.6.4","RL.6.9","RI.6.1","RI.6.6","RI.6.9","RL.7.1","RI.7.1"];const WRITE_STD=["W.6.1","W.6.2","W.6.3","W.7.2"];const MATH_STD=["6.G.A.2","6.SP.B.5","7.SP.B.4","7.G.B.6"];const EXT_MATH=["6.RP.A.3","6.NS.A.1","6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6","6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c","6.EE.C.9","6.G.A.1"];const TEXT_STD=READ_STD.concat(["W.6.8","W.7.8"]);const dl=a=>`<dl>${a.map(x=>`<dt>${x[0]}</dt><dd>${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function wt(st){return st.std.includes("W.6.8")||st.std.includes("W.7.8")?"notes":st.std.includes("SL.6.4")?"say":st.std.includes("W.6.3")?"story":st.std.includes("W.6.1")?"argue":"explain"}const G_STORY=[["flash drive","a small stick that stores computer files"],["slideshow","a set of pictures shown one after another on a screen"],["on a loop","playing over and over again automatically, with no beginning or end"],["program","the printed order of what happens at an event"],["chorus","a group that sings together"]];const G_GYM=[["reverberation","sound bouncing off hard surfaces and arriving late"],["reverberation time","the number of seconds a sound takes to fade away in a room"],["absorb","to soak up sound instead of sending it back"],["consonant","a speech sound that is not a vowel, such as t, k or s"],["intelligibility","how easily speech can be understood"],["acoustical engineer","someone whose job is measuring and fixing sound in rooms"]];const G_ARG=[["point of view","where the writer stands on the subject"],["purpose","what the writer is trying to get done"],["connotation","the feeling a word carries beyond its plain meaning"],["imperative","a sentence that gives an instruction"],["account","one person's written report of what happened"],["emphasize","to give something more weight than the rest"]];const G_DATA=[["observation","one piece of data in the set"],["attribute","the thing being measured"],["median","the middle value when the data is in order"],["mean","the total divided by the number of values"],["quartile","the value a quarter of the way through the ordered data"],["interquartile range","the third quartile minus the first quartile"],["distribution","the shape the data makes when you plot it"],["striking deviation","a value that sits far away from all the others"]];const G_VOL=[["volume","the amount of space inside a solid, counted in cubic units"],["unit fraction cube","a cube whose edge is a unit fraction, such as 1/2 or 1/4"],["base area","the area of the face the solid stands on"],["surface area","the total area of all the faces, in square units"]];const G_WRITE={argue:[["claim","what you want the reader to believe or do"],["alternate claim","the strongest thing the other side would say"],["relevant evidence","facts that actually bear on the claim"],["formal style","no slang, no texting language, no jokes to the reader"]],explain:[["domain-specific vocabulary","the technical words a subject uses"],["transition","a word or phrase that shows how two ideas connect"],["concrete detail","a specific fact, number or example rather than a general statement"]],story:[["point of view","who is telling the story"],["pacing","how fast or slow the story moves through its events"],["sensory language","words for what can be seen, heard, felt, smelled or tasted"],["reflection","a sentence where the narrator says what the events meant"]],notes:[["credible","able to be trusted"],["plagiarism","using someone else's words or ideas as if they were your own"],["paraphrase","to say it in your own words"],["citation","the line that says where a fact came from"],["search terms","the words you type to find sources"]],say:[["claim","what you are telling the council to do"],["finding","something the evidence shows"],["weakness","the soft spot in your own case"]]};function glossHtml(st){if(st.std.includes("6.SP.B.5")||st.std.includes("7.SP.B.4"))return dl(G_DATA);if(st.std.includes("6.G.A.2")||st.std.includes("7.G.B.6"))return dl(G_VOL);if(st.id===3||st.std.includes("RI.7.1"))return dl(G_GYM);if(st.id===6||st.id===8||st.id===10)return dl(G_ARG);if(st.id===11||st.std.includes("W.7.8"))return dl(G_WRITE.notes.concat(G_GYM));if(st.id===12)return dl(G_WRITE.say.concat(G_ARG));if(st.id===13)return dl(G_WRITE[wt(st)]||G_WRITE.explain);return dl(G_STORY)}const TERMS=[["cite","point to the exact words in the text that support your answer"],["evidence","the words that prove it, not words on the same topic"],["inference","something the text does not say outright but that the clues support"],["theme","an idea about life that a whole text is working on"],["genre","a kind of writing, such as a story, a poem or a report"],["tone","the attitude the writing takes toward its subject"],["connotation","the feeling a word carries beyond its plain meaning"],["point of view","where the writer stands on the subject"],["presentation","what one writer chooses to include, leave out and emphasize"]];const FR={cite:["The text says, “____.”","I can infer that ____, because the text says “____.”","The strongest evidence is “____,” because ____."],"RL.6.4":["The word ____ suggests ____, while ____ would suggest ____.","The writer chose ____, which makes the tone ____.","This phrase means ____, and I know because ____."],"RL.6.9":["Both are about ____.","The story approaches it by ____, while the poem ____.","The story ends with ____. The poem ends with ____."],"RI.6.6":["The author's point of view is ____.","Her purpose is ____.","The words “____” show it, because ____."],"RI.6.9":["Both accounts agree that ____.","Account A includes ____, which ____. Account B leaves it out.","A calls it “____” while B calls it “____.”"],"SL.6.2":["The report recommended ____.","The chart adds ____.","Together they show ____."],"6.SP.B.5":["There are ____ observations. The attribute is ____, measured in ____.","The median is ____ and the mean is ____.","Most of the data ____, but ____ is a striking deviation.","The ____ describes a typical value better, because ____."],write:["My claim is ____.","One reason is ____. The evidence is ____.","Somebody might object that ____. However, ____.","For these reasons, ____."]};function framesHtml(st){const k=st.std.includes("RL.6.4")?"RL.6.4":st.std.includes("RL.6.9")?"RL.6.9":st.std.includes("RI.6.6")?"RI.6.6":st.std.includes("RI.6.9")?"RI.6.9":st.std.includes("SL.6.2")?"SL.6.2":st.std.includes("6.SP.B.5")||st.std.includes("7.SP.B.4")?"6.SP.B.5":WRITE_STD.some(x=>st.std.includes(x))||st.std.includes("SL.6.4")?"write":"cite";return`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.2rem">${FR[k].map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function gramHtml(){return`<h4>Pronoun case</h4>`+dl([["Subjective","I, we, he, she, they, who. Use it when the pronoun does the verb."],["Objective","me, us, him, her, them, whom. Use it after a verb or a preposition."],["Possessive","my, our, his, her, their, whose."]])+`<h4>Two tests that settle most of it</h4>`+ul(["Take the other person out: “gave it to Dani and I” becomes “gave it to I,” which is wrong, so it is me.","Find the verb: if the pronoun is doing it, use the subjective case."])+`<h4>Pronoun problems to watch for</h4>`+dl([["Shift in person","do not start with a student and finish with you"],["Shift in number","a singular noun such as committee takes a singular pronoun"],["Vague pronoun","if her could be either girl, name her instead"],["Intensive pronoun","herself, themselves. Adds emphasis, and the sentence still works without it."]])+`<h4>Phrases and clauses</h4>`+dl([["Prepositional phrase","a preposition plus its noun: after eight weeks of meetings"],["Subordinate clause","has a subject and a verb but cannot stand alone: although the chorus had finished"],["Misplaced modifier","a describing phrase next to the wrong noun. Move it beside what it describes."]])}const FORM={"6.SP.B.5":["Summarizing a data set",ul(["<strong>Number of observations:</strong> how many values are in the set.","<strong>The attribute:</strong> what was measured, how it was measured, and the units.","<strong>Median:</strong> put the values in order and take the middle one. With an even count, average the middle two.","<strong>Mean:</strong> add all the values and divide by how many there are.","<strong>Quartiles:</strong> the median of the lower half and the median of the upper half, leaving the overall median out when the count is odd.","<strong>Interquartile range:</strong> third quartile minus first quartile.","<strong>Pattern and deviation:</strong> where the data bunches, and any value far away from the rest.","A single extreme value moves the mean a long way and the median hardly at all."])],"6.G.A.2":["Volume with fractions",`<p class="formula">V = l × w × h = B × h</p>`+ul(["Write each mixed number as an improper fraction first: 3 1/2 becomes 7/2.","Multiply the tops, multiply the bottoms, then simplify.","<strong>Packing:</strong> count how many unit fraction cubes fit along each edge, multiply the three counts, then multiply by the volume of one cube.","A cube with edge 1/2 has volume 1/8. A cube with edge 1/4 has volume 1/64.","<strong>A missing edge:</strong> volume ÷ the area of the face you know.","Volume is in cubic units."])],"7.G.B.6":["Volume and surface area",`<p class="formula">V = l × w × h</p><p class="formula">Area of a triangle = 1/2 × base × height</p>`+ul(["A box has three pairs of matching faces, so find three areas and double each one.","Surface area is in square units. Volume is in cubic units.","For a prism on any base: volume = area of the base × the length."])],"7.SP.B.4":["Comparing two data sets",ul(["Compare centers with the median, or the mean when neither set has an extreme value.","Compare spread with the interquartile range or the range.","A difference between two centers only means something if it is large next to the spread inside each group."])]};FORM["7.SP.B.4"]=FORM["7.SP.B.4"];FORM["6.RP.A.3"]=["Ratio, rate and percent",ul(["A ratio compares two amounts. Simplify it by dividing both by the same number.","A percent is a rate out of 100: part &divide; whole, then multiply by 100.","To scale a rate up, find what the whole was multiplied by and multiply the part by the same number."])];FORM["6.NS.A.1"]=["Dividing fractions",`<p class="formula">a/b &divide; c/d = a/b &times; d/c</p>`+ul(["Ask what the division means: how many of the second fraction fit inside the first.","Check by multiplying: the quotient times the divisor has to give the dividend back."])];FORM["6.NS.C.5"]=["Positive and negative",ul(["Opposite directions take opposite signs: over and under, above and below, owed and saved.","Zero is the point the two directions are measured from. Say what it is before you write the sign."])];FORM["6.NS.C.6"]=["Points and opposites",ul(["Further right on the number line is greater, even among negatives.","The opposite of a number is the same distance from 0 on the other side, and the opposite of the opposite is the number itself.","(x, y): across first, then up. Reflecting across the y-axis changes the sign of x; across the x-axis changes the sign of y."])];FORM["6.EE.A.1"]=["Exponents",ul(["An exponent counts how many times the base is used as a factor, so 2^5 = 2 x 2 x 2 x 2 x 2.","Work the exponent before any multiplication, division, addition or subtraction."])];FORM["6.EE.A.2c"]=["Evaluating an expression",ul(["Put the value in for the letter, then work in the conventional order: brackets, exponents, multiply and divide, add and subtract.","3n^2 means 3 times n squared, not (3n) squared.","Volume of a cube V = s^3, in cubic units. Surface area A = 6s^2, in square units."])];FORM["6.EE.C.9"]=["Two quantities that change together",ul(["The independent variable is the one you choose. The dependent variable is the one that follows from it.","Write the dependent variable alone on the left: m = 6n.","The number multiplying the independent variable is how much the dependent one changes for each step."])];FORM["6.G.A.1"]=["Areas of polygons",`<p class="formula">Triangle = 1/2 &times; base &times; height</p><p class="formula">Trapezoid = (base 1 + base 2) &divide; 2 &times; height</p>`+ul(["For an L shape, either cut it into rectangles and add, or take the missing corner off the whole rectangle.","Area is always in square units."])];function formHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>{if(seen.includes(FORM[x][0]))return"";seen.push(FORM[x][0]);return`<h4>${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`}).join("")}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){let e=calcE.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-");if(!/^[0-9+\-*/().\s]*$/.test(e)||!e)return"";try{const o=(e.match(/\(/g)||[]).length-(e.match(/\)/g)||[]).length;if(o>0)e+=")".repeat(o);const v=Function('"use strict";return ('+e+")")();return Number.isFinite(v)?String(+v.toPrecision(10)):"Error"}catch(x){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","(","1","2","3","−",")","0",".","+","C","="];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Four functions only. For a fraction, divide the top by the bottom.</p>`}function calcBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.slice(0,-1);calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k}r()})}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}const PLAN={argue:["My claim","Reason 1 and the evidence","Reason 2 and the evidence","The strongest objection, and my answer","Conclusion"],explain:["My topic","Fact group 1","Fact group 2","Terms I need to define","Conclusion"],story:["Who, where and when","What was planned","What actually happened","What came of it afterwards"],notes:["Which source I trust, and why","My search terms","My quote or paraphrase","My citation"],say:["My recommendation","Finding 1","Finding 2","The weakness in my case","What I need from the council"]};function planHtml(st){const S=E.state;S.plan=S.plan||{};const k=wt(st),v=S.plan[k]||[];return`<div class="planner">${PLAN[k].map((x,i)=>`<label>${x}<textarea data-pi="${i}">${(v[i]||"").replace(/</g,"&lt;")}</textarea></label>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Your plan is saved for the speaking stop and the writing stop.</p>`}function planBind(b,r,st){b.querySelectorAll("[data-pi]").forEach(t=>t.oninput=()=>{const S=E.state,k=wt(st);S.plan=S.plan||{};S.plan[k]=S.plan[k]||[];S.plan[k][+t.dataset.pi]=t.value;E.save()})}const SENT={argue:{s:["Linden Park should ____ because ____.","The survey shows ____.","Somebody might object that ____. However, ____.","For these reasons, ____."],w:["because","for example","however","although","as a result","by contrast","therefore"]},explain:{s:["A gym is hard to hear in because ____.","First, ____.","As a result, ____.","____, which means ____.","In short, ____."],w:["because","as a result","however","for example","by contrast","in short"]},story:{s:["I had planned ____.","At first, ____.","Then ____.","“____,” ____ said.","Afterwards, ____."],w:["at first","then","after that","later","by the time","in the end"]},notes:{s:["I trust Source ____ because ____.","These search terms are best because ____.","Dr. Priyanka Raval reports, “____.”","According to Dr. Priyanka Raval, ____.","Author. “Title.” Publisher, year."],w:["according to","reports","found","states"]},say:{s:["Linden Park should ____.","My first finding is ____.","My second finding is ____.","The weakness in my case is ____.","From the council I need ____."],w:["because","the survey shows","compared with","by contrast","for that reason"]}};function sentHtml(st){const d=SENT[wt(st)]||SENT.explain;return`<ul class="starters">${d.s.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul><h4>Linking words</h4><div class="wordbank">${d.w.map(x=>`<span>${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`}const CHECK={argue:["I stated my claim.","I gave reasons with evidence from the fact box.","I used the numbers accurately.","I named the strongest objection and answered it.","I used linking words.","I kept a formal style.","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."],explain:["I named my topic.","I grouped related facts together.","I used facts and details from the article.","I defined every technical term I used.","I used transitions.","I kept a formal style.","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."],story:["I set the scene and said who is telling it.","Events are in an order that makes sense.","I used dialogue or description.","I used time words.","I used precise, sensory details.","My ending reflects on what happened.","I reread it for mistakes."]};function checkHtml(st){return`<ul class="card-list" style="list-style:none;padding-left:0">${(CHECK[wt(st)]||CHECK.explain).map(x=>`<li><label style="display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:flex-start"><input type="checkbox" style="width:20px;height:20px;margin-top:4px"> ${x}</label></li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const TOOLS={gloss:{name:"Word help",std:TEXT_STD.concat(WRITE_STD,MATH_STD,["SL.6.2","SL.6.4"]),html:glossHtml},terms:{name:"Reading terms",std:READ_STD,html:()=>dl(TERMS)},frames:{name:"Answer frames",std:READ_STD.concat(WRITE_STD,MATH_STD,["SL.6.2","SL.6.4"]),html:framesHtml},gram:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.6.1","L.7.1"],html:gramHtml},form:{name:"Formula and method card",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD.concat(EXT_MATH),html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},calc:{name:"Calculator (four function)",std:MATH_STD.concat(EXT_MATH),html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},plan:{name:"Planner",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.6.8","W.7.8","SL.6.4"]),html:planHtml,bind:planBind},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.6.8","W.7.8","SL.6.4"]),html:sentHtml},check:{name:"Writing checklist",std:WRITE_STD,html:checkHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"passage",std:READ_STD,msg:"If the text was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",std:["W.6.8","W.7.8","SL.6.4","W.6.1","W.6.2"],msg:"If you read the fact box, the source cards or the table aloud, note it. The checks on this stop still stand."},{t:"gloss",msg:"Word help explains background words such as reverberation, consonant and quartile. It does not give answers, but note that it was used."},{t:"gloss",std:["RL.6.9"],msg:"Word help defines theme and genre, so count the naming part of the theme question as supported and rate the idea the student states."},{t:"gloss",std:["6.SP.B.5","7.SP.B.4"],msg:"Word help defines median, mean, quartile, interquartile range and striking deviation, so this stop then shows computing and interpreting rather than recalling the terms."},{t:"terms",std:READ_STD,msg:"The reading terms card defines cite, evidence, inference, theme, genre, tone, connotation and point of view, so the stop shows applying those ideas with a reference."},{t:"frames",msg:"Answer frames give the shape of the answer. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:["6.SP.B.5","7.SP.B.4"],msg:"The frame for this stop lists the parts of a data summary in order, so count the completeness of the summary as supported and rate whether each figure is right."},{t:"frames",std:["RI.6.9"],msg:"The frames name agreement, selection and wording as the three things to compare, so count the sorting part of the answer as supported."},{t:"gram",std:["L.6.1","L.7.1"],msg:"The grammar card gives the pronoun cases, both tests, the pronoun problems and the phrase types, so this stop shows applying a stated rule rather than recall."},{t:"form",std:["6.SP.B.5","7.SP.B.4"],msg:"The method card gives the steps for median, mean, quartiles and interquartile range and states how an extreme value moves each measure, so count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"form",std:["6.G.A.2","7.G.B.6"],msg:"The card gives V = lwh, V = Bh, the volume of a half and a quarter unit cube, and the surface area method, so this stop shows applying a stated formula rather than recall."},{t:"calc",std:MATH_STD,msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic. Rate whether the student set the problem up correctly and whether the answer carries the right unit."},{t:"mult",std:MATH_STD,msg:"The multiplication chart supplied the facts. Rate the setup and the reasoning."},{t:"mult",std:EXT_MATH,msg:"The multiplication chart supplied the facts. Rate the setup and the reasoning."},{t:"calc",std:EXT_MATH,msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic. Rate whether the student set the problem up correctly and whether the answer carries the right unit."},{t:"form",std:EXT_MATH,msg:"The method card states the rule or the formula this stop is built on, so these answers show applying a stated method rather than recalling it. Note it."},{t:"plan",msg:"The planner supplies the organization, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"plan",std:["W.6.1"],msg:"The planner includes a line for the strongest objection, so count the counterclaim check as supported."},{t:"sent",msg:"Sentence frames supply the linking words and the closing, so count those checks as supported."},{t:"check",msg:"The checklist names what to include. Note that the student used it to revise."},{t:"scribe",msg:"If an adult wrote, rate the ideas and the organization. Do not rate conventions the adult supplied."},{t:"fewer",msg:"If questions were skipped, change the “of” number in the score to the number the student tried."},{t:"hint",msg:"Note which question got the hint, and count that question as supported."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:TEXT_STD}}});
